WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.690
- Welcome to the Lifespan Podcast.

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.640
Where we discuss the science of aging

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.893
and how to be healthier
at any stage of life.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:10.880
I'm David Sinclair.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.550
I'm a professor of Genetics
at Harvard Medical School,

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.330
and co-director of the Paul F. Glen Center

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.540
for Biology of Aging Research.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.840
This series of podcasts
is about why we age

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.750
and things we can do to slow, stop,

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:26.070
and even reverse that process.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:26.903
Last episode,

00:00:26.903 --> 00:00:29.780
we talked about really
cutting-edge science

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.010
and procedures that
people are currently doing

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.050
with their doctors

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.360
and what they might be
doing in the coming years.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.380
But today, we're going to
talk about what you can do

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:39.650
to slow, stop,

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:43.810
and even reverse the aging
of the outside of your body.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:48.810
And I'm joined today by my
lovely co-author and co-presenter

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.350
- Co-human.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.930
Co-ed.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:52.763
No.

00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:54.520
- Conspirator?
- Co-conspirator.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:55.700
Matthew LaPlante.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.870
Welcome.
- Hey.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.393
All right, let's do this again.

00:00:59.528 --> 00:01:00.361
You know this.

00:01:00.361 --> 00:01:02.170
I was not so excited about this episode.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.560
When you told me,

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.440
when Wonderboy Rob told us

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.200
that it was going to be
about cosmetic aging.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.060
I poo-pooed this, then we prepped for it.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:12.530
We've been having great conversations

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.580
over the last few days.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.560
I'm actually really excited
about this conversation.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.620
- That's true. Especially the parts about

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.330
improving your sexual function.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.010
I thought you were excited about the most.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.990
- That's not in this episode.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.610
We'll have an episode on that.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.740
No, today we're talking
about cosmetic aging,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.040
and we're talking about the difference

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.730
between interventions that
are only aimed at appearance

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:35.540
and interventions that might impact

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.120
both appearance and aging.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.270
- And we had to cover a number of topics,

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:44.040
including how to keep your hair,
skin and nail looking young

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.640
and even reversing
aspects of that process.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.473
- And we should say here,

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:51.270
there's nothing wrong with
things that are purely aesthetic

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.313
that only work to make
ourselves look good.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.410
Because those things might not
impact our biological aging,

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:03.400
but they can affect us
socially, emotionally, mentally,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.430
like it will make our lives better.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.080
So we're not going to poo-poo.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.120
I've used the word
poo-poo now several times.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.700
So we're not going to denigrate

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.890
interventions that are
only aimed at appearances,

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.523
but we are going to talk more about

00:02:15.523 --> 00:02:18.300
and center our focus around interventions

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.280
that might also have an anti-aging effect.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.640
- Exactly. And there are coverups.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.390
We're going to to talk
a little bit about Botox

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:26.920
and other creams.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.590
But really I think what's most exciting

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.140
and most powerful

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.710
are the technologies that are
available now and shortly,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.680
that can boost the body's
ability to fight aging

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:38.870
and even reverse it.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.140
We put them into a couple of buckets.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.120
We've talked about this
in previous episodes.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.550
Is the adversity mimetics which we love.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.400
Which is really slowing
down aging and reversing it.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.990
Or the abundance mimetics that
give you a short term gain,

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:51.060
make you look good,

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.067
but long term may not work or
even have deleterious effects.

00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:57.530
- Now this is usually
the part in the podcast

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.130
where I say, "Hey, David, we
have to thank our sponsors."

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.730
But actually there's
something else we need to do

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.210
because there was something
we neglected to talk about

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.653
in our last episode.

00:03:09.007 --> 00:03:11.420
Well, it was a pretty glaring
oversight on our part.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.100
- Well, we focused a lot on testosterone,

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:16.140
which is used by men
and women increasingly,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.503
but we neglected to
talk about other hormone

00:03:18.503 --> 00:03:21.320
that are not found much in men.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.930
Estrogen, progesterone,

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:26.340
and this pertains to
menopause hormonal therapy

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.820
and overall hormone
replacement therapy for women.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:31.840
So let's talk about that.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:32.673
- Yeah.

00:03:32.673 --> 00:03:36.580
And one of the reasons why this is really

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.410
both interesting and important

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:43.410
is because menopause effects
the first part of our body,

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:48.410
the first organ in a woman's body

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.320
to really experience the
effects of biological aging.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.480
- Right. Sometimes even
in women's twenties,

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.150
but late thirties, early
forties, this can occur.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:01.410
- So this is often before your
heart starts to show damage,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.580
your lung starts to
show damage from aging,

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.170
your brain, even.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:06.180
- Yeah. And there are ways

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.150
of making sure that you
go through that transition

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.630
if you're a woman.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:12.140
In a more natural way.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:13.840
And so we actually called
up a friend of mine,

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.660
Cindy Meston, who's a professor in Austin

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.910
who has given us some really good advice.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:20.440
Given that you and I are men,

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.590
we typically don't know a lot about this.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.200
- And should not lecture on it.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.033
- No, we're not lecturing,

00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:27.870
but we will talk about
how it pertains to aging.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.980
What the current thought
about HRT actually is.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.770
- Well, can you take us through,

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.287
we had this conversation several years ago

00:04:33.287 --> 00:04:34.550
and it stuck with me
for a really long time.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:36.760
Can you talk about why it is

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:40.580
that the female reproductive
system ages earlier

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.740
than the rest of the body?

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.110
You started to notice this in mice

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.330
- Yeah. In fact, that's one of the reasons

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:46.910
we studied fertility in mice

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.810
because we could get those
experiments done more quickly.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:49.940
And we discovered,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.280
and we'll bring this
up in a later episode,

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.020
that we can reverse female infertility

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.990
with molecules that stimulate.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:57.540
These were two ones that we work on.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.910
But today we're going to talk
about the late part of life

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.670
about how to replace those hormones.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.770
The reason you need to do that

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.010
is that we've evolved as a species

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:09.560
to have children up until
the age of about 30.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:10.700
And then we should stop. Why?

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.800
Because having kids as a
woman is very dangerous.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.180
There was a time when,

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.010
I think there was a 1 in 5 chance

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.600
you would die from childbirth.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.010
- You needed to be
younger or more vigorous

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.840
to just get through that process.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:22.673
- Right.

00:05:22.673 --> 00:05:23.630
And then you have to put a lot of effort

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:24.730
into raising those kids.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:25.563
It's not like an insect

00:05:25.563 --> 00:05:27.020
where you just let them
blow away in the wind.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.410
You have to spend at least a decade.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.400
And these days, two
decades, raising those kids.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.110
And if you keep doing
that your whole life,

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:36.860
it might take away from the energy

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.450
that you put into the ones that you have.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:40.700
So for that reason,

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:45.700
women have evolved to become
infertile around the age of 40.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:49.433
- And that process that they
go through is menopause.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:54.880
For some women it's brutal.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:56.700
- For sure. There's the well known things.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:00.120
There's mood swings, vaginal dryness,

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.590
loss of sex drive, migraines,

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:04.010
hot flashes, of course.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:06.300
There's also really
serious effects on health,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.100
including increased
susceptibility to heart disease.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.820
So this is clearly something
that needs to be addressed.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.300
And there are increasingly better ways

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.330
to be able to allow women

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.440
to not just transition through menopause,

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.490
but to continue having
the right hormone levels

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.820
to protect them against aging long-term.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:26.430
- And one of these things is
menopausal hormone therapy,

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.990
which is chiefly and has
been traditionally chiefly

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:30.110
estrogen replacement.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:34.460
Although increasingly other
hormones are explored.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.870
And this is a treatment
that can offer people

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.700
a lot of symptomatic relief.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.970
There were concerns for a really long time

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.740
that it had some pretty
gnarly side effects itself,

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.270
and even some could do
some long term damage.

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:49.030
We're learning more about
that right now, though.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:51.050
- Estrogen used to be used primarily.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.210
And now, what we're finding

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:54.850
is that the dual use of
estrogen and progesterone

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:58.020
in the right combination
and ratio and timing

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:58.853
is really important.

00:06:58.853 --> 00:07:00.500
And it may be that some
of these side effects

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.520
that people have been worried about

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.020
and studies that came out early,

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.590
particularly in the early 2000s

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:07.910
about the increased risk of cancer

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.720
are largely due to,

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:11.810
I wouldn't say the misuse,

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:14.300
but the lack of understanding

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.740
of what the natural
levels are for a woman.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:17.800
In fact, most women don't know

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:19.080
what their baseline levels are

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.440
in their thirties and forties.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:21.810
That's why Cindy actually,

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:24.190
Professor Meston, recommended to us

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:26.680
that women should get
their baseline levels

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:31.280
and measure during certain
phases of their menstrual cycle,

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.210
particularly in the luteal phase,

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.840
what the levels are of
estrogen and progesterone

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:36.673
in their thirties and forties,

00:07:36.673 --> 00:07:40.090
so that when they reach
their late forties, fifties,

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:42.260
their physician can match those

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.950
and continue the on at those
natural levels for them.

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:47.060
- And this continues a
common theme that we've had

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.400
in I think almost every
discussion we've had

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:50.340
so far in this series,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.280
which is unless you're monitoring,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.340
unless you're tracking,

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:58.350
you have no way of knowing what
your actual health state is.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.070
- Right. And so I've
been using InsideTracker,

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:00.930
one of our sponsors.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:02.860
You can use a doctor if you want.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:05.300
But that's true not just for
estrogen and progesterone,

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:06.270
but for testosterone.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:09.120
Men and women typically
don't measure their levels.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:10.110
They should be.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:15.110
And there's a sex hormone
binding globulin called SHBG,

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:15.983
which is important

00:08:15.983 --> 00:08:19.720
for binding all these proteins
in the blood, these hormones.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:21.670
And if you don't measure that one as well,

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:23.280
you're flying blind with your dashboard.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.260
So I would recommend
measuring that as well.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.990
And if the levels of that become too high,

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:28.650
SHBG it's called,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.740
then that can actually
counteract these therapies.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.240
- Okay. And so, again,

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:35.220
at the risk of lecturing on things

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.720
like body parts that we don't have,

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.210
suffice it to say a lot of the things

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:40.043
that people have been hearing

00:08:40.043 --> 00:08:42.170
about menopausal hormone
therapy for many years,

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:46.410
the dangers of it
shouldn't scare people off

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.580
from having conversations
with their physicians

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:52.070
about this potential treatment.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:52.903
- Exactly.

00:08:52.903 --> 00:08:56.160
And even increasingly so as physicians

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.840
and researchers like Cindy,

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.660
are figuring out the
right ratio and timing

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:01.993
of these hormones.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:04.070
Increasingly, it's looking

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:06.250
like there's a real long-term protection.

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:08.360
So not just during the years of menopause,

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.780
but extending that hormone
replacement therapy

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:11.880
out for decades

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.410
seems to protect against
the heart disease problems

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:15.530
and bone loss.

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:16.880
But of course, there are some downsides,

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:18.470
particularly if you have a
history of breast cancer.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:20.580
You don't want to be pumping
yourself with estrogen.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.010
So please do talk to your
physician about that.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.510
- We mentioned a list
of effects that happened

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.510
during menopause earlier.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:29.570
One of the ones that we didn't mention

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:31.660
that relates to the theme of this episode

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.220
is epidermal thinning.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:36.860
- Right. This is what happens
to your skin as you get older

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:38.980
and it's accelerated rapidly

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:41.237
during and especially after menopause.

00:09:41.237 --> 00:09:43.070
- And we're going to talk
about that more in a moment,

