00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.950
A lot of people, I think, know this.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:05.290
This fact, but it still, I think,

00:13:05.290 --> 00:13:06.700
surprises some people.

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.670
The largest organ in the human body is

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:10.503
- The skin.

00:13:10.503 --> 00:13:13.370
- And it's sizable.

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:16.300
- Well, it's 1/6 of
your total body weight,

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:17.133
which is massive.

00:13:17.133 --> 00:13:19.210
And it's the barrier to the world.

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:22.120
And the health of your
skin is super important

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:24.500
for the health of the rest
of your body and vice versa.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:27.120
- And monitoring the health of your skin

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:32.120
can give you a lot of insights
into the health of your body.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:33.930
When we were talking before,

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:36.180
I likened this to going
to the grocery store

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.510
and looking at a piece of fruit.

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:38.950
- Right. If you see some bruises

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:41.160
and some damage on there,

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.500
you can assume that it
wasn't handled very well,

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:46.120
but also it might be old.

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:47.920
So you give it a squeeze,
you see how it is.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:50.310
And we'll talk about ways to
do that with our own bodies

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:53.565
to really have a quick test
and even some molecular tests

00:13:53.565 --> 00:13:57.150
to determine how molecularly
old we actually are.

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:58.890
- There's one you've been
doing all morning this morning.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:00.270
Do you want to do it now?

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:01.310
- The skin pinch test.

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:02.270
- Yeah.

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:03.490
- Sure.

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.780
So what you do is you
rest your hand on a table,

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:07.450
so it'd be relaxed normally.

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:09.100
And then you grab the back of your skin,

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:10.360
pinch it up,

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:12.240
and it should, if you're young,

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.190
pop straight down in less than 2 seconds.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:16.200
Someone in their thirties and forties,

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:18.323
it'll pop straight down. But
once you get into your fifties,

00:14:18.323 --> 00:14:19.980
it's going to take many seconds.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:22.320
- And the chart that you found

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:25.670
and I don't know what the
scientific basis for this is,

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:27.090
but the chart that you found said

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:29.150
when you get into your
like eighties and nineties,

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:32.100
it can take, well, like 20, 30 seconds?

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:32.933
Something like that.

00:14:32.933 --> 00:14:34.150
- Oh. Even worse.

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:35.580
So for people like us,

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:37.010
were in our forties and fifties,

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:39.180
should be less than 10 seconds.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.010
By the time you're 60,

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.920
it typically takes 10 to
15 seconds to come down.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:48.540
Over 70, between half and
a whole minute to go down.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.660
- Okay. And you're 50 and
yours went down right away.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:51.650
So that's good.

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:52.990
- Well, I've been doing the right stuff

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:53.823
for a number of years,

00:14:53.823 --> 00:14:55.926
but that's pretty good for
someone my age, looks like yours

00:14:55.926 --> 00:14:57.583
- We'll also going to
fix that on the video.

00:14:58.840 --> 00:14:59.673
Yeah.

00:14:59.673 --> 00:15:01.630
It also helps if you tense your hands.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:04.860
- We don't probably even need to say this,

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:06.620
but like, it's just obvious.

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:08.790
I am aging faster than you are aging,

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:12.110
but I'm doing okay on
the the skin popup test.

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:13.420
- You are.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:15.170
There are other ones we
can talk about later,

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:16.470
like nail growth.

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:18.030
- Let' stick on skin for
a minute here though.

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:20.500
Because one of the things we talked about

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:24.640
right before the sponsorship
break was epidermal thinning.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:28.930
This is a problem for women who
are suffering from menopause

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:30.760
It's also a problem for men.

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:34.080
It's a problem for people as they age.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.040
And I think a lot of
people don't understand

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:36.970
or don't really think about it,

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:38.000
at least, I didn't think about

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:41.420
why this is such a pernicious problem.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:44.000
Why is it bad if we have really thin skin?

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:45.940
In addition to the fact that
we can't take insults well.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:46.773
- Right.

00:15:46.773 --> 00:15:47.750
Well, we tend to forget

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:50.760
that our skin is the
major organ in our body

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:52.860
to protect us from death.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:54.880
You know, we've seen with COVID-19

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.690
what happens is once you
get a pathogen in there,

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.370
it'll run rampant.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:01.250
And so our skin is the main barrier.

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:04.200
And as we get older, it can
be damaged much more easily.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.210
You see an elderly people,

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:06.960
the bruising, the ripping, the tearing.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:09.260
It's becoming paper thin.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:10.710
And so we need to protect it.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:13.210
It's not just about looking good

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:14.560
in your forties, fifties, sixties,

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:16.310
but in your seventies,
eighties and nineties,

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:17.480
it's super important.

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:18.670
It's life and death

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:21.190
as to how young your skin still is.

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:25.410
- This really hit home for me
when I met David Armstrong.

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:29.310
- Yeah. So he's the US's top surgeon

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:32.280
for ulcers, wounds in the foot.

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:34.680
And he spends his whole life

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.610
trying to figure out ways to
reduce the number of ulcers,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:41.550
which is about 13% of the US
has a foot ulcer right now

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:43.180
that's about
- Like right now.

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:44.592
- 40 million Americans have foot ulcers.

00:16:44.592 --> 00:16:46.523
- Yeah.
- And we don't talk about it.

00:16:46.523 --> 00:16:48.390
Cause these are people
who typically are older

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:51.040
and don't have a voice
in society, typically.

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:53.190
But he is on a mission

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:57.660
to advocate for the cure of these things.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:16:59.650
And also he spends his life

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:02.190
trying to cure people of these ulcers.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:03.170
But it's very difficult.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:04.540
The reason is these ulcers,

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:07.150
because the skin is old,
it doesn't heal very well.

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:09.530
And typically it'll be open.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:12.060
A weeping wound and
crusty around the outside.

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:14.990
It looks like you've
got a quarter size hole

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:15.870
in the bottom of your foot.

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:19.310
That just arises just by
tripping on something,

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:21.203
or stepping on a something
sharp that's layering it out.

00:17:21.203 --> 00:17:23.710
- But if it's coupled diabetes,

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:27.040
which leads to a loss of sensation,

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:28.170
of feeling in the foot,

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:29.910
this can be incredibly dangerous

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:33.250
and lead to among other
things, amputation.

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:36.890
Infection, which leads
back to this whole reason

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:38.450
why we're talking about
why it's so important

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:43.450
to keep our skin as thick
and healthy as possible.

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:45.390
- And keep the feeling in our skin.

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:47.370
Remember, our nerves are all in our skin.

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:48.203
And as you get older,
you lose that feeling.

00:17:48.203 --> 00:17:49.420
And there's a story

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:51.070
that we wrote about in our book, Lifespan,

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:53.240
where one of David Armstrong's patients

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:56.310
had stepped on a thumbtack, a little nail,

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:58.270
and it was in his foot, he didn't notice.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:17:59.620
And he only noticed days later

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:01.710
because he could hear a tapping
sound as he was walking.

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:02.543
- It's absolutely terrible.

00:18:02.543 --> 00:18:04.450
Every time I hear that
story, I just cringe.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:05.570
- Well, it's a really big problem

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:07.870
because often you can't make these heal.

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:10.730
And then what David does
and all these other surgeons

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:13.740
is to cut around that scabby site.

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:15.280
And it just gets bigger
and bigger and bigger.

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:17.550
And eventually you've
just got this open wound.

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:19.870
Next thing you have to
do is cut the foot off.

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:22.380
And then you keep cutting and cutting.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.280
I think after about five years

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:26.070
of getting one of these big ulcers,

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:27.820
you are almost certainly going to die.

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:29.920
It's worse than getting
a cancer diagnosis.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:31.460
- And there's a lot of people
probably listening right now

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:33.317
and go, "Oh no, this is
never going to happen to me.

00:18:33.317 --> 00:18:34.983
I don't have to worry about
that. I'm super healthy.

00:18:34.983 --> 00:18:36.290
I'm not going to get diabetes."

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:39.070
But we're all aging.

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:42.900
And I think most people can
picture their elder self.

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:45.800
And when they do, they see their skin,

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:47.930
it's a little more wrinkly,
it's a little more saggy.

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:49.730
It's a little thinner.

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:52.110
And this actually means something

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.543
more than just appearances.

00:18:55.690 --> 00:18:57.550
And we know this from this 2012 study,

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:59.900
where researchers took the photos

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.340
of about 300 elderly people

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:06.680
and they gave them to another
cohort of people to rate.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:09.220
They rated for the age.

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:11.970
- Is that the Lothian
Birth Cohort in Edinburgh?

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:12.803
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:19:12.803 --> 00:19:13.830
- All right.

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:17.150
So they looked at people from 1921 onwards

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:18.770
and it was fascinating.

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:20.380
They rated them for a bunch of things,

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:21.490
their age, their health,

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.580
attractiveness, facial symmetry.

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:24.940
- This is just based on the appearance

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:25.773
on the photograph by the way.
- Right.

00:19:25.773 --> 00:19:27.500
- Like, no, you're not
meeting these people.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:28.460
Were not taking any tasks,

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:29.860
we're just looking at their photos

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:31.970
and saying, "Are they healthy or not?

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:34.040
Are they old or not?"

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:35.010
- So what did they find?

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:39.530
- They found that if you
follow those people out

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:43.210
seven years from the time
that that photo was taken,

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:46.520
there was a really high
degree of predictability

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:48.840
that the people who were
rated as older looking,

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:50.680
whether or not they were actually older

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:51.770
chronologically or not,

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:54.760
if they were older looking,
they were more likely to die.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:55.760
- Well, that totally fits

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:59.590
with what I hear from my friend,
Nir Barzilai in New York.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:01.920
He's at the Albert Einstein
College of Medicine.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:03.470
And he studies centenarians.

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:04.890
These are families, peoples

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:07.250
that tend to live over a hundred.

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:08.780
Not to be confused with centurions,

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:09.850
which are Roman soldiers.

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:12.120
Sometimes that's mixed up.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:14.000
But what he sends me sometimes

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:15.800
are photos of his centenarians,

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:16.940
the father and the son.

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:21.530
And both those guys that
now imagining in my head

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:22.490
look really young.

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:24.670
Not just the guy that's a
hundred who probably looks 70,

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:27.150
but the 70 year old son looks 40.

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.180
And so this is fitting with the idea

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:31.653
that you are really as old as you look.

00:20:33.310 --> 00:20:37.033
- When I read about the 2012 study,

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:38.983
I immediately thought,

00:20:38.983 --> 00:20:40.540
"Oh, you know what would
be really interesting?

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:42.370
Is if now we could take those photos

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:45.570
and then test those
people's biological ages

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:48.280
with a DNA methylation clock.

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:49.293
- A Horvath clock.

00:20:49.293 --> 00:20:52.110
- Horvath clock, which we
talked about in prior episodes.

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:54.580
And lo and behold, somebody
had already thought of this.

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:55.840
This often happens, by the way.

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:56.870
You and I are talking about research

00:20:56.870 --> 00:20:58.190
and we like, "Oh, we
should check this out."

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:00.210
And then somebody else has done it.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:02.250
But another team of researchers did do it.

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:05.590
And it turns out that it didn't correlate.

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:09.180
There wasn't correlation between
these people's appearances,

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:14.180
even though that was predictive
and their biological clock,

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:18.100
but there's a distinction
that needs to be made.

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:21.010
- Well, they were measuring
the blood clock in that case.

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:22.830
So what we've learned since

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:25.400
is that there are various
tissues have different clocks.

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:27.220
And it helps to have a specific clock

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:29.190
for that particular tissue.

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:31.890
Others have since gone
on to make skin clocks.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:33.130
And they seem to work better

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:34.880
for measuring the age of the skin.

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:37.100
- And Mariana Boroni

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:40.710
who's a friend and colleague
of yours from Brazil.

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:43.620
She let a study that
was published in 2020,

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:44.910
showing that when you do this,

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:46.420
when you build this epigenetic clock

00:21:46.420 --> 00:21:50.530
based on methylation of skin cells,

00:21:50.530 --> 00:21:54.110
it does predict biological age quite well.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:54.943
- It does.

00:21:54.943 --> 00:21:57.340
And there's a good reason
why skin is going to age

00:21:57.340 --> 00:21:59.250
probably faster than the rest of the body,

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:00.390
though it's still a good indicator.

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:02.540
And that is that it's
exposed the elements.

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:05.870
We know that if you grow up
in Australia, like I did,

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:08.470
that the UV light is
going to create DNA damage

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:11.540
that accelerates the epigenetic
changes that lead to aging.

00:22:11.540 --> 00:22:14.650
So someone like me is going
to have probably older skin

00:22:14.650 --> 00:22:16.930
than someone, my identical twin,

00:22:16.930 --> 00:22:19.290
if they had moved to Norway.

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:23.280
- And like a third of Australians
suffer from some sort of,

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:25.390
is it skin cancer?

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:27.260
- Yeah. It's skin cancer.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:29.020
It doesn't have to necessarily be lethal.

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:30.530
But a third of Australians

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:32.240
have something removed from their skin

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:34.310
that could be a tumor.

00:22:34.310 --> 00:22:36.830
Now, what's been done since I was a kid

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:39.990
is that all beaches have free sunscreen,

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:41.100
which is great.

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:42.290
Not in my day where we
need to just sunbathe.

00:22:42.290 --> 00:22:44.710
- Like when you show up to the
beach, there's like a little,

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:46.744
- Yeah. Yeah. The press pump.

00:22:46.744 --> 00:22:48.270
Instead of having ketchup in the US,

00:22:48.270 --> 00:22:49.290
they have sunscreen.

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:51.120
Well, now we have the hand sanitizer.

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:51.953
But it's the same thing, right?

00:22:51.953 --> 00:22:52.786
It's like,
- Don't use that.

00:22:52.786 --> 00:22:54.343
- So that's at the beaches?
- It is.

00:22:54.343 --> 00:22:55.435
- You don't have to
bring your own sunscreen.

00:22:55.435 --> 00:22:56.268
- No, no, no.

00:22:56.268 --> 00:22:57.880
- That would save so much money.

00:22:57.880 --> 00:22:58.713
Cause that sounds expensive.

00:22:58.713 --> 00:23:01.030
- Billions of dollars in
healthcare costs in a country

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:02.670
for just a few dollars of sunscreen.

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:04.560
- That's a really great
investment actually.

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:06.960
- Yeah. Sometimes Australia
does things really well.

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:10.320
But the problem though is that,

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:12.930
for those of us who grew
up without the sunscreen,

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:15.870
we have this damage that
we are probably stuck with,

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:17.440
though increasingly we're learning

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:19.850
you might be able to
reverse that damage as well.

00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:24.850
- And that was part of this
study by Mariana Boroni as well.

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:29.740
They looked at the
application of senolytic drugs

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:32.560
to these skin cells

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:34.590
and then measured the results.

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:36.820
- They did. So let's talk about skin aging

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:38.780
from a really modern perspective,

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:41.470
which is that skin is
full of senescent cells.

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:43.300
These are zombie-like cells

00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:46.490
that reach a point where their
epigenome is so dysregulated

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:47.820
that they check out of the system,

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:49.440
they stop dividing.
- They're no longer producing.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:51.170
- Right. They don't divide

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:52.360
and we can stain them blue

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:54.630
with a dye called beta-galactosidase.

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:58.630
And they're pretty common
in an older person.

00:23:58.630 --> 00:24:01.820
The problem is that they secrete
these inflammatory factors

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:04.830
and also what are called
mitogens that stimulate cancer.

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:05.720
So they're not good things.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.430
And in mouse and increasingly
in a few human studies,

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:11.460
we know that if you delete
these cells, kill 'em off,

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:13.790
you get rejuvenation of the body.

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:16.970
It includes internal organs,
you can treat diabetes with it.

00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:19.960
But the skin becomes rejuvenated.

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:20.793
And in this study,

00:24:20.793 --> 00:24:23.690
what they did was they took
out skin biopsies from people

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:27.320
and grew them in tissue culture
dishes, so it's ex vivo,

00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:31.790
and they could then treat
them with senolytic drugs.

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:33.530
They used a couple, a pretty common ones.

00:24:33.530 --> 00:24:36.890
One's called ABT-263
which is from Abbott's,

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:38.830
also known as Navitoclax.

00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:40.320
And they also used rapamycin,

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:41.590
which we've talked about before

00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:44.540
as a way of mimicking
starvation in the body

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:45.890
or fasting at least.

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:48.990
And what they found was
that those skin explants,

00:24:48.990 --> 00:24:50.440
as they're called, grew thicker,

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:52.797
which is what you want if
you're reversing aging,

00:24:52.797 --> 00:24:55.270
but they also could measure the clock.

00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:58.430
They had a special skin clock
that they had built themselves

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:01.200
out of measuring the DNA
methylation chemical patterns

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:03.360
during aging of skin.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:06.080
And there, they did find
that this senolytic treatment

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:09.510
reversed the age of those
little pieces of skin.

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:12.100
- The implications of
what you're talking about

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:14.570
in regards to the senescent
cells are really interesting.

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:17.020
Because again, what we're talking about

00:25:17.020 --> 00:25:20.860
is the body's largest organ.

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:23.270
And it's not a system unto itself,

00:25:23.270 --> 00:25:25.120
it interacts with all
of these other systems.

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:26.793
So if we have senescent cells

00:25:26.793 --> 00:25:30.340
secreting these cytokines
all over the place.

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:31.440
They're not just staying in the skin,

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:33.310
they're going to move other places too.

00:25:33.310 --> 00:25:36.240
So if you can lower the age of the skin,

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:38.350
you can save the rest of the body.

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:39.630
- That's exactly right.

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:41.140
And James Kirkland from the Mayo Clinic

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:43.520
has found that if you
just put what we call it,

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:47.000
what he called a dab of
senescent cells under the skin,

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:48.070
in a mouse,

00:25:48.070 --> 00:25:50.240
it causes the whole mouse to get older.

00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:52.770
So lowering the overall
burden of senescent cells

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:54.030
is a good thing.

00:25:54.030 --> 00:25:56.590
And where are most
senescent cells in the body

00:25:56.590 --> 00:25:58.820
because of the damage from UV light,

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:00.840
you get them mostly in the skin.

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:03.480
- Okay. So one of the ways
to do this potentially,

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:05.050
to fix this problem potentially

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:06.900
is retroactively,

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:08.970
we can address it with
some kind of senolytics.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:10.430
We can also do it proactively

00:26:10.430 --> 00:26:13.360
by just simply avoiding all that UV light,

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:14.593
wearing sunscreen.

00:26:15.756 --> 00:26:17.750
Let's talk for a little bit

00:26:17.750 --> 00:26:19.720
about what's actually happening.

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:24.050
How does UV light actually
like make ourselves go bonkers?

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:27.970
- Right. Well, the first
thing to appreciate is,

00:26:27.970 --> 00:26:29.490
a little bit of sunlight
isn't going to hurt you.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:31.060
In fact, you need it.

00:26:31.060 --> 00:26:32.910
- Vitamin D.
- It's not bad for you.

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:35.160
But you can overwhelm the system.

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:37.400
So if you actually get
sunburn, you get red,

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:38.610
there's inflammation,

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:40.430
swelling of the skin, tenderness,

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:42.130
then you know you've overdone it.

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:45.800
And so what I tend to do is I
go out a little bit each day,

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:48.210
maybe for 10, 20 minutes,
get a bit of sun,

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:50.230
but not to the extent where
I'm going to get burnt.

00:26:50.230 --> 00:26:52.430
And I always wear sunscreen out there

00:26:52.430 --> 00:26:54.430
if I'm going to be out
for longer than that.

00:26:54.430 --> 00:26:55.820
So what's happening actually

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:59.170
is that the sunlight is
hitting the DNA molecule itself

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:01.860
and causing two letters in the DNA,

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:03.370
two chemicals, the Ts.

00:27:03.370 --> 00:27:05.360
Remember there's ACTG, four of them.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:08.220
The Ts that are next to each
other will link chemically.

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:10.210
It's called a, a thymine-thymine dimer.

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:11.150
And now the cell,

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:12.980
- If the sun fuses them?

00:27:12.980 --> 00:27:13.950
- The sunlight. Yeah, yeah.

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:16.660
Photon will hit them and
they'll become fused.

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:17.564
Like that.

00:27:17.564 --> 00:27:19.230
[Matthew laughing] And so
if you've got a zipper,

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:21.150
you can imagine what's
happened to a zipper

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:22.990
that's fused along.

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:25.080
How do you possibly open up that zippers?

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:27.730
So you need to open up DNA
to read it and to copy it.

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:30.210
And now you've got these stuck together.

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:32.070
- So you can't. They
can't be opened up in it.

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:35.200
- Well, it's a big bulge in
the enzymes that read DNA,

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:36.490
have trouble getting over it.

00:27:36.490 --> 00:27:37.410
So you have to remove them.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:40.190
Usually what happens is
that the cell cuts them out

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:41.720
and puts new ones back in.

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:42.830
- Okay. And when you do,

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:44.060
when you kind of unfuse those,

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:45.500
do you know what happens?

00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:46.970
You live long and prosper.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.080
- That also helps. Hang on.
Let's see if I can do that.

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:50.570
There we go.

00:27:50.570 --> 00:27:52.480
So what's actually happening though,

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:53.860
is not what you might think.

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:57.160
The old idea was that
these thymine diamonds

00:27:57.160 --> 00:27:59.870
are actually mutagenic
and cause mutations.

00:27:59.870 --> 00:28:03.280
But what we now know from
studies from my lab and others

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:06.230
is that the act of repairing that DNA

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:07.690
damages the problem.

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:10.440
And it's the reorganization
of the proteins in the cell

00:28:11.620 --> 00:28:15.110
that ultimately lead to the
cell losing its identity.

00:28:15.110 --> 00:28:17.670
- Okay. So I have not been
as good as you have been

00:28:17.670 --> 00:28:19.863
about wearing sunscreen.

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:25.690
So I have to probably
address this retroactively.

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:30.690
Can I just like rub some
rapamycin and ABT-263 on my skin

00:28:30.700 --> 00:28:31.580
and it's all good?

00:28:31.580 --> 00:28:32.550
- It probably would work.

00:28:32.550 --> 00:28:33.383
You have to be careful

00:28:33.383 --> 00:28:35.738
cause rapamycin suppresses
the immune system, remember?

00:28:35.738 --> 00:28:36.720
- Right.
- And right now,

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:38.420
those drugs are not
available, as far as I know,

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:40.220
for topical treatment.

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:44.310
But actually, Dr. Boroni,
then took that finding

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:48.340
and then use that little
skin explant beta-gal stain,

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:49.430
which is blue,

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:51.900
to look for peptides,

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:53.670
small little proteins that we talked about

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:55.600
in the last episode.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:58.750
Which one of those 700 and
something peptides, if any,

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:00.480
had age reversal effects

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:02.710
got rid of that blue senescent stain

00:29:02.710 --> 00:29:04.040
and reversed the clock?

00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:06.600
And out of those 700
and something peptides,

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:07.830
she found a few.

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:09.610
There were about 20 that looked good.

00:29:09.610 --> 00:29:12.490
And one of them she's
actually put into a product

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:13.980
that she sent me to try the other day.

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:14.813
It's called OneSkin.

00:29:14.813 --> 00:29:17.960
And I'm not an advisor to
this group or anything,

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:19.390
but it did seem to work.

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:21.170
It looked just as good as retinae,

00:29:21.170 --> 00:29:22.490
which is the leading product

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:25.340
that is used to reduce fine
lines and wrinkles too.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:27.820
But I know she's developing
a additional peptides

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:28.910
to put into her cream,

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:31.980
not just this one that is
starting to show some efficacy

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:33.407
in human clinical trials.

00:29:33.407 --> 00:29:34.970
- And the thought process

00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:37.660
behind having a peptide
in a cream is what?

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:38.570
- So we don't know exactly

00:29:38.570 --> 00:29:40.180
how this peptide makes the skin younger,

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:41.357
but one of the downstream effects,

00:29:41.357 --> 00:29:45.450
one of the benefits certainly
is involving the COL1A1 gene

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:47.570
that makes the protein collagen,

00:29:47.570 --> 00:29:49.630
which we've known for over 50 years

00:29:49.630 --> 00:29:52.090
to be an important
component of younger skin,

00:29:52.090 --> 00:29:53.650
making it more flexible and thicker.

00:29:53.650 --> 00:29:54.483
- Collagen, of course,

00:29:54.483 --> 00:29:57.040
is really super popular supplement

00:29:57.040 --> 00:29:59.733
to put in beauty products right now.

00:29:59.733 --> 00:30:01.410
It's a protein,

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:06.170
but it starts to attenuate
after our mid-twenties.

00:30:06.170 --> 00:30:07.430
- Right.

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:08.263
When we're young,

00:30:08.263 --> 00:30:09.760
it's the most abundant
protein in the body.

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:11.713
It holds together our bones and our skin.

00:30:11.713 --> 00:30:14.070
- This is why babies are all chubby

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:16.010
and beautiful and plump, right?

00:30:16.010 --> 00:30:17.240
- Yeah. It's a wonderful protein,

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:18.780
but we don't make enough
of it as we get older.

00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:21.240
And that's why beauty
products in particular,

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:22.610
want to boost that production.

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:25.290
But you can also smear it on,

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:27.120
put it in powders, put it in drinks,

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:28.390
take it as a pill.

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:30.220
Energy drinks have it in it.

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:31.570
The question though is,

00:30:31.570 --> 00:30:33.300
is it useful?

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:35.460
Is eating collagen going to be good?

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:36.670
Well, one thing I can say for sure

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:39.470
is that collagen is made
up of a lot of amino acids.

00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:43.110
You're going to get the right
ratio of collagen amino acids

00:30:43.110 --> 00:30:44.570
from those products

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:46.640
whether or not the collagen itself

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:48.200
makes it into your bloodstream

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:50.489
after being degraded in your stomach.

00:30:50.489 --> 00:30:51.670
- By your stomach acids.

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:52.503
- Yeah.

00:30:52.503 --> 00:30:53.370
There's a big debate

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:55.830
about whether these products are working

00:30:55.830 --> 00:30:57.330
by being broken down and rebuilt.

00:30:57.330 --> 00:30:59.290
Or actually make it into the body

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:02.220
as an intact peptide of
string of amino acids.

00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:04.290
And I would say the jury's well and truly

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:06.233
still out on those things.
- It is fair to say

00:31:06.233 --> 00:31:08.640
that as long as you pick
a safe collagen product,

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:11.940
there's likely not a lot of
harm that can come from it.

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:13.060
- Not at all.

00:31:13.060 --> 00:31:14.660
There's no harm in taking collagen.

00:31:14.660 --> 00:31:16.150
And probably what it's making sure

00:31:16.150 --> 00:31:17.800
is that you don't have any deficiencies

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:18.890
in the amino acids you need

00:31:18.890 --> 00:31:20.980
to make this most abundant
protein in the body.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:23.178
- What we know right now

00:31:23.178 --> 00:31:25.690
is that there's probably more benefit

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:30.690
from retinol or vitamin C in
terms of protecting your skin.

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:34.160
- Well, yeah. Vitamin C is
good for overall body's health.

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:37.250
It's essential as an antioxidant.

00:31:37.250 --> 00:31:41.490
Retinae though is one of
the most important aspects

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:43.840
for preventing and reversing fine lines

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:45.080
and wrinkles in the skin.

00:31:45.080 --> 00:31:48.740
- And these substance
actually create collagen

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:50.020
down the line, right?

00:31:50.020 --> 00:31:52.530
- Right. These retinoids have
been shown for many years

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:54.750
to boost the production of collagen.

00:31:54.750 --> 00:31:56.060
That's clear.

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:56.960
They also do other things.

00:31:56.960 --> 00:31:58.920
They increase the growth of the epidermis

00:31:58.920 --> 00:31:59.950
and the stratum corneum,

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:01.750
which are important parts of the skin,

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:03.020
make it thicker.

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:07.280
They also can actually lower
skin pigmentation by about 60%.

00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:10.253
A lot of people want to
have even colored skin.

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:12.780
They make fibroblasts grow,

00:32:12.780 --> 00:32:14.270
basically stimulating cell growth,

00:32:14.270 --> 00:32:16.060
which is really important.

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:16.893
They lower the lipid.

00:32:16.893 --> 00:32:20.920
So that's why it's often used
as a defense against acne.

00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:22.460
And in fact, you were telling me earlier

00:32:22.460 --> 00:32:23.700
that you've used that before.

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:25.600
- when I was a kid. Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:27.389
- It dried out my skin
something awful though.

00:32:27.389 --> 00:32:29.690
I mean, it makes you like
super susceptible to sunlight.

00:32:29.690 --> 00:32:31.760
So you have to wear
sunscreen when you use it.

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:33.510
- Well, that's true today.

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:35.160
If you put it on your skin as an adult,

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:37.040
you have to be careful from the sun,

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:37.873
but mostly you end up with dry skin.

00:32:37.873 --> 00:32:40.270
So you have to make
sure you're moisturizing

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:42.410
while you use the retinol products.

00:32:42.410 --> 00:32:44.823
And there's another thing
that's important about these.

00:32:44.823 --> 00:32:46.330
They are what are called MMPs,

00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:50.040
this metalloproteinases
that break down the skin

00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:51.660
and you want to inhibit those as well.

00:32:51.660 --> 00:32:53.480
Which is what retinols do.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:55.317
Actually, what they do is
they go inside the cell

00:32:55.317 --> 00:32:57.440
and there's a receptor inside the cell

00:32:57.440 --> 00:32:59.860
that binds to the chemical
that you're adding.

00:32:59.860 --> 00:33:01.730
And they then go in and
turn on certain genes

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:04.360
that provide all of this extra cell growth

00:33:04.360 --> 00:33:07.543
and other benefits to the skin.
But you have to be careful

00:33:07.543 --> 00:33:09.930
because if you overdo it,
you get really dry skin.

00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:12.150
And if you get it into
places like your eye,

00:33:12.150 --> 00:33:13.200
it can be really unpleasant.

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:14.510
- Oh god, that sounds awful.

00:33:14.510 --> 00:33:16.080
- Yeah, don't do that.

00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:18.750
- They also have an
advantage of creating keratin

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:20.060
or promoting keratin?

00:33:20.060 --> 00:33:23.410
- Right. So the keratinocytes
are lower down in the skin.

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:26.040
So keratin is a super
abundant molecule as well.

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:28.940
That's important, like
collagen, for the flexibility.

00:33:28.940 --> 00:33:31.060
It's an important component
of not just your skin,

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:32.990
but your hair and your nails as well.

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:37.960
And your retinae can promote
the growth of the keratinocytes

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:39.140
that make keratin.

00:33:39.140 --> 00:33:41.350
- Let's take a little aside
here and talk about nails.

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:43.180
Because this is sort of like,

00:33:43.180 --> 00:33:46.040
you know, skin, hair, nails.

00:33:46.040 --> 00:33:49.590
And a lot of people forget
that this is a pretty good,

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:51.377
or like a lot of people might not know

00:33:51.377 --> 00:33:55.840
that this is a pretty good
indicator of your state of aging.

00:33:55.840 --> 00:33:57.420
- Yeah. It's a pretty fun one.

00:33:57.420 --> 00:33:59.530
I learned this a number of
years ago when I was at MIT,

00:33:59.530 --> 00:34:00.960
that the rate of your nail growth

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:04.010
is a really good indicator of
how you're aging or not aging.

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:06.260
And so every time I have to cut my nails,

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:08.840
I'm thinking, how long
ago did I cut my nails?

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:11.510
It's all born from a study from 1979,

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:14.090
where they glued on these
little measuring tapes

00:34:14.090 --> 00:34:15.320
onto people's nails

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:17.300
and measured them over years, in fact,

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:18.943
and there was one individual,

00:34:20.180 --> 00:34:21.160
Mr.Bean actually,

00:34:21.160 --> 00:34:22.382
who measured his nails
for most of his life.

00:34:22.382 --> 00:34:23.760
- Like literally his name was Mr. Bean?

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:24.593
- Yeah.
- Like the,

00:34:24.593 --> 00:34:25.426
- He might have been Dr. Bean

00:34:25.426 --> 00:34:27.060
or Professor Bean, I'm not sure.

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:27.980
But we have that graph.

00:34:27.980 --> 00:34:29.850
And actually, looking
at thousands of people,

00:34:29.850 --> 00:34:33.620
we now know for sure that
the rate of nail growth

00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:36.210
decreases about half a percent per year.

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:37.500
And if you measure that,

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:38.880
it's a really good indicator

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:41.840
of how old you are biologically.

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:42.673
I don't do that,

00:34:42.673 --> 00:34:43.770
but I do pay attention

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:46.050
to whether my nails are slowing
down in their growth or not.

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:47.830
- I don't because I bite my nails,

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:49.540
which I know is a terrible habit,

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:52.710
but I like to say now that I'm
just engaging in autophagy,

00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:54.800
which is, you know, good for my lifespan.

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:56.090
- Right. Taking that keratin

00:34:56.090 --> 00:34:57.470
and putting it back in your stomach.

00:34:57.470 --> 00:34:58.303
- Right. Exactly.

00:34:58.303 --> 00:35:03.303
- But autophagy actually
is a function also

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:06.090
or a result also of retinae.

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:08.190
- It is. There's Ana Maria Cuervo

00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:09.730
down at Albert Einstein
College of Medicine

00:35:09.730 --> 00:35:10.563
is a world leader,

00:35:10.563 --> 00:35:12.610
probably the world leader
in understanding autophagy,

00:35:12.610 --> 00:35:15.180
the recycling of old proteins in the cell.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:17.490
There's shallow autophagy
when you skip meals,

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:19.010
there's deep cleansing,

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:21.350
what's called
chaperone-mediated autophagy,

00:35:21.350 --> 00:35:23.490
which is after three days of fasting,

00:35:23.490 --> 00:35:27.420
these retinoic acids control
that pathway of autophagy

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:29.560
through a protein called LAMP2A.

00:35:29.560 --> 00:35:31.390
What's exciting about Ana Maria's work

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:32.310
is just this year,

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:35.560
she published that if you
upregulate that mechanism,

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:38.380
this chaperone-mediated autophagy,

00:35:38.380 --> 00:35:39.213
mice live a lot longer.

00:35:39.213 --> 00:35:41.070
30, 40% longer.

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:43.430
So it's truly a longevity mechanism

00:35:43.430 --> 00:35:45.330
that we can control with these retinoids.

00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:47.600
- But there's something a
little bit counterintuitive here

00:35:47.600 --> 00:35:51.760
because retinae actually
turns out to maybe not engage,

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:53.700
It's connection with the autophagy

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:57.730
isn't necessarily positive for lifespan

00:35:57.730 --> 00:36:00.090
like we think in other cases.

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:02.150
- Right. As we keep
saying in these episodes,

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:05.730
there's a balance between
hunkering down during adversity,

00:36:05.730 --> 00:36:08.740
and mimicking that and
going hell-for-leather,

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:10.270
burning the candle at both ends

00:36:10.270 --> 00:36:11.940
with these abundance mimetics.

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:14.880
And it looks like retinoids, retinae

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:17.880
are the second type that they
stimulate the body to grow

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:20.750
because the body thinks
it's a time of abundance.

00:36:20.750 --> 00:36:23.830
But it might be at the expense
of long-term skin health.

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:26.620
Because what it's also doing
is toning down, shutting down

00:36:26.620 --> 00:36:29.530
this CMA, chaperone-mediated autophagy

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:30.840
that is shown, at least in animals,

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:32.330
to extend their lifespan.

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:37.330
- So retinae long-term bad
for the aging of our skin?

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:39.664
Jury is out?

00:36:39.664 --> 00:36:40.650
- Unclear, unclear.
- Unclear.

00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:42.930
- So what I would say as a scientist,

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:45.170
might work, though it needs to be tested,

00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:48.670
is use retinols if you want
to reduce wrinkles and lines,

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:49.750
but not every day.

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:50.610
Give your body a rest.

00:36:50.610 --> 00:36:53.450
Perhaps when you're fasting, don't use it.

00:36:53.450 --> 00:36:57.220
And so you get that cleansing
in between the growth periods.

00:36:57.220 --> 00:36:58.850
- Okay. So let's talk about one more thing

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:03.160
that everybody associates
with cleansing antioxidants.

00:37:03.160 --> 00:37:04.250
But I know you're like,

00:37:04.250 --> 00:37:07.520
at least in terms of
like oral antioxidants,

00:37:07.520 --> 00:37:08.860
you're not a big fan.

00:37:08.860 --> 00:37:09.910
You don't believe that they work

00:37:09.910 --> 00:37:11.380
the same way that people think they work.

00:37:11.380 --> 00:37:12.810
What about when they're in skin creams?

00:37:12.810 --> 00:37:14.750
Cause you see that all the time.

00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:18.160
Every skin cream's got like,
"Loaded with antioxidants."

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:19.580
- Right. So you said, what I believe.

00:37:19.580 --> 00:37:21.040
Well often I don't use that term believe

00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:22.050
because I'm a scientist

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:23.810
and I like to report
what's in the literature.

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:26.110
And what's in the
literature on antioxidants

00:37:26.110 --> 00:37:28.490
is kind of depressing for longevity.

00:37:28.490 --> 00:37:29.980
Over the last 30 years,

00:37:29.980 --> 00:37:32.300
a lot of different
antioxidants have been tried.

00:37:32.300 --> 00:37:34.270
Ingesting them, injecting them,

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:35.620
genetically modifying mice,

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:37.120
putting it on skin.

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:38.750
Very few results, if any.

00:37:38.750 --> 00:37:40.120
There is one antioxidants

00:37:40.120 --> 00:37:42.340
that seems to work
pretty well for longevity

00:37:42.340 --> 00:37:44.160
and that's resveratrol.

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:44.993
But as you know,

00:37:44.993 --> 00:37:47.210
I don't believe resveratrol
is working primarily

00:37:47.210 --> 00:37:48.960
through its antioxidant activity.

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:51.480
Instead, it's activating
our body's defenses

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:53.180
and mimicking adversity.

00:37:53.180 --> 00:37:54.610
- So there are topical creams

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:56.090
that have resveratrol in them now?

00:37:56.090 --> 00:37:58.350
- There are an increasing
number of cosmetics

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:00.310
that have resveratrol in them.

00:38:00.310 --> 00:38:02.670
But resveratrol is probably
not working primarily

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:04.120
through its antioxidant activity.

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:06.240
It's pretty weak as an antioxidant.

00:38:06.240 --> 00:38:07.720
What's likely happening is

00:38:07.720 --> 00:38:10.130
the resveratrol is turning
on the sirtuin activity,

00:38:10.130 --> 00:38:12.770
which then turns on antioxidant enzymes,

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:13.860
but a lot of other good things,

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:16.640
revving up metabolism,
increasing blood flow,

00:38:16.640 --> 00:38:19.120
getting oxygenation
into the tissue as well.

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:20.040
So I think that resveratrol

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:23.010
is a really great component of a cosmetic,

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:25.050
but not for the reasons
that you might think.

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:28.210
- Are there other NAD-boosting molecules

00:38:28.210 --> 00:38:30.130
that are in skin creams right now?

00:38:30.130 --> 00:38:31.330
- Well, I'm actually working on a way

00:38:31.330 --> 00:38:32.780
to boost NAD in the skin.

00:38:32.780 --> 00:38:35.030
That product's not
ready for primetime yet,

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:36.320
but already people have figured out

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:39.190
that if you boost NAD levels,
it's good for the skin.

00:38:39.190 --> 00:38:42.070
Often you find nicotinamide
or nicotinic acid

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:45.710
or molecules related
to those in cosmetics.

00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:46.543
And they've been shown

00:38:46.543 --> 00:38:48.730
to actually have quite
beneficial effects on the skin.

00:38:48.730 --> 00:38:49.930
Thickening, for example.

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:53.210
Mostly, people think it's
because of the B vitamin,

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:54.043
vitamin B3,

00:38:54.043 --> 00:38:55.350
but actually, probably what's going on

00:38:55.350 --> 00:38:58.590
is it's turning on again the
sirtuin defenses of the skin.

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:00.500
- There's one more molecule
we should talk about

00:39:00.500 --> 00:39:02.470
as we're talking about topographic creams

00:39:02.470 --> 00:39:04.620
before we move on to other remedies.

00:39:04.620 --> 00:39:07.643
That's hyaluronic acids, HA.

00:39:08.620 --> 00:39:10.910
These are really, really
popular right now.

00:39:10.910 --> 00:39:12.230
- Well, yeah. They're really important

00:39:12.230 --> 00:39:16.300
for the plumpness of the
skin and the ability to flex.

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:17.640
So when you do that,

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:21.000
this is collagen, but
it's also HA involved.

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:22.700
And it's not just in the skin,

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:24.550
HA is found throughout the body.

00:39:24.550 --> 00:39:26.400
And what's super interesting about HA

00:39:26.400 --> 00:39:29.960
is that it's found in abundance
in long-lived organisms.

00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:32.230
It seems to be involved
in protecting animals,

00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:34.520
in particular, from cancer.

00:39:34.520 --> 00:39:37.820
One organism we've talked about
before is the naked mole rat

00:39:37.820 --> 00:39:41.440
Actually, one of our children, Alex,

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:44.930
is handling and helping
to raise naked mole rats

00:39:44.930 --> 00:39:46.490
up at the University of Rochester.

00:39:46.490 --> 00:39:48.410
So I get photos of these naked mole rats.

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:50.050
They have beautiful skin by the way.

00:39:50.050 --> 00:39:50.883
Nice and smooth.

00:39:50.883 --> 00:39:54.940
I think we call them a
condom filled with walnuts?

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:56.070
- In an earlier episode,

00:39:56.070 --> 00:39:58.610
we did refer to them as a
condom filled with walnuts,

00:39:58.610 --> 00:40:01.060
which does not at least invoke in my mind,

00:40:01.060 --> 00:40:02.120
a beautiful animal.

00:40:02.120 --> 00:40:03.780
- But it's very smooth and supple.

00:40:03.780 --> 00:40:04.933
- Beauty is subjective.

00:40:06.377 --> 00:40:07.960
- Moving right along,

00:40:07.960 --> 00:40:10.070
what's found is if you
take out these cells

00:40:10.070 --> 00:40:11.130
from the naked mole rats,

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:13.140
and this is Vera Gorbunova's work

00:40:13.140 --> 00:40:15.240
where Alex works in the lab.

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:16.630
Those cells,

00:40:16.630 --> 00:40:17.480
they were found to produce

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:19.630
this jelly-like substance in the dish.

00:40:19.630 --> 00:40:21.040
And when she looked at what it was,

00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:22.820
it was hyaluronic acid.

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:23.653
And then she found out

00:40:23.653 --> 00:40:26.820
that if you take away
that hyaluronic acid,

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:28.110
the cells will divide

00:40:28.110 --> 00:40:30.590
and become more tumorigenic or cancerous.

00:40:30.590 --> 00:40:33.020
Conversely, what she's
doing now is making a mouse

00:40:33.020 --> 00:40:35.750
that has heaps of
hyaluronic acid in its body,

00:40:35.750 --> 00:40:37.810
with the expectation and hope

00:40:37.810 --> 00:40:39.720
that it will be resistant to cancer.

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:41.020
Long way of saying

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:42.770
that these products
aren't just for beauty,

00:40:42.770 --> 00:40:44.940
they might actually be preventing cancer

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:45.930
in the skin as well.

00:40:45.930 --> 00:40:49.740
- Your colleagues at
Harvard Medical School,

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:52.100
Kristina Liu and Janelle Nassim

00:40:52.100 --> 00:40:54.470
have said that topical HA

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:56.450
does work really well as a moisturizer.

00:40:56.450 --> 00:40:57.550
But they've suggested

00:40:57.550 --> 00:41:01.130
that if the goal is to improve volume loss

00:41:01.130 --> 00:41:03.340
and laxity of the skin,

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:06.220
that you might want to
consider an injectable HA,

00:41:06.220 --> 00:41:07.450
does that make sense to you?

00:41:07.450 --> 00:41:08.283
- Well, it does.

00:41:08.283 --> 00:41:11.700
Cause HA is typically a
big chain of chemicals.

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:13.230
It's sugars put together

00:41:13.230 --> 00:41:14.430
and they don't get into the skin

00:41:14.430 --> 00:41:15.360
unless they're really small.

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:18.330
So one way of doing it is
injecting it into the actual skin.

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:20.710
But even then it's hard for
the cells to take it up.

00:41:20.710 --> 00:41:23.010
So one other way that's in some cosmetics

00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:25.030
is to have small chains of HA.

00:41:25.030 --> 00:41:26.930
Oligomers, they're called,

00:41:26.930 --> 00:41:28.170
to let them get into the skin

00:41:28.170 --> 00:41:31.320
and then let the skin
synthesize those chains

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:34.950
inside the cell where they
belong and also outside.

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:36.440
That can be done naturally.

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:38.600
But also what we discovered
in patented in my lab

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:42.340
is that resveratrol boosts the HAS2 gene,

00:41:42.340 --> 00:41:45.090
which makes the enzyme that
makes those strands of HA.

00:41:45.090 --> 00:41:47.630
- David, a lot of things
we've discussed so far.

00:41:47.630 --> 00:41:50.150
At first, they seem purely cosmetic,

00:41:50.150 --> 00:41:52.450
but then when we've dive a little deeper,

00:41:52.450 --> 00:41:55.340
we realized there were some
anti-aging implications.

00:41:55.340 --> 00:41:58.690
So I didn't want to move on from skincare

00:41:58.690 --> 00:41:59.523
until we talked about

00:41:59.523 --> 00:42:03.403
what is probably the
most popular intervention

00:42:04.790 --> 00:42:08.040
for wrinkles, for aging skin,

00:42:08.040 --> 00:42:09.190
and talk about whether or not

00:42:09.190 --> 00:42:13.980
there are any anti-aging
implications in Botox.

00:42:13.980 --> 00:42:15.480
- Right. Well, besides retinol,

00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:17.690
Botox is the most popular treatment.

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:20.180
And let's talk about what Botox is.

00:42:20.180 --> 00:42:21.100
I think most people know,

00:42:21.100 --> 00:42:22.890
but let's just remind everybody.

00:42:22.890 --> 00:42:25.800
It's a toxin that comes from a bacterium,

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:27.670
clostridium botulinum.

00:42:27.670 --> 00:42:29.690
It's found in foods.

00:42:29.690 --> 00:42:31.539
If you eat it, a lot of it,

00:42:31.539 --> 00:42:32.407
So you don't need much.
- You're about to lose 'em.

00:42:32.407 --> 00:42:33.980
- Nanograms will kill you.

00:42:33.980 --> 00:42:35.690
In fact, if you have, what is it?

00:42:35.690 --> 00:42:37.070
A gram or kill a million people,

00:42:37.070 --> 00:42:39.040
and 2 kilos can kill all of humanity.

00:42:39.040 --> 00:42:40.310
It's that toxic.

00:42:40.310 --> 00:42:42.140
- And we inject this into our faces?

00:42:42.140 --> 00:42:45.303
- We do. And it's very effective
at getting rid of wrinkles.

00:42:45.303 --> 00:42:47.000
There's no question.

00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:50.250
The way it works is it
inhibits neurotransmission.

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:52.690
And neurons are telling the muscles in,

00:42:52.690 --> 00:42:54.090
let's take my forehead, for example,

00:42:54.090 --> 00:42:57.220
are pinching off the
muscles, they're tighten,

00:42:57.220 --> 00:42:58.260
and you get the wrinkles.

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:01.480
So if you get Botox in there,
the muscles will relax.

00:43:01.480 --> 00:43:05.010
And then the wrinkles will go
away for six to nine months.

00:43:05.010 --> 00:43:06.090
You have to keep repeating it though

00:43:06.090 --> 00:43:08.210
and keep getting injected in those spots.

00:43:08.210 --> 00:43:11.930
- But this is not a clearing
out of cellular senescence

00:43:11.930 --> 00:43:16.010
or REX, differentiation of cells.

00:43:16.010 --> 00:43:18.810
This is just numbing the nerves.

00:43:18.810 --> 00:43:21.890
- Right. It's purely
cosmetics, as far as we know.

00:43:21.890 --> 00:43:24.030
It's not medical.

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:27.140
It's not treating headaches,
which it can be used for.

00:43:27.140 --> 00:43:27.973
But that's okay.

00:43:27.973 --> 00:43:29.610
If you look good, you're
going to feel better.

00:43:29.610 --> 00:43:31.100
You're going to have a better social life.

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:32.180
All of that's good for longevity.

00:43:32.180 --> 00:43:33.610
So it's still worth talking about.

00:43:33.610 --> 00:43:36.020
- Okay. Let's talk about skin peels now.

00:43:36.020 --> 00:43:37.620
Same thing? No real benefit?

00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:39.330
I mean, it just make your skin look nice

00:43:39.330 --> 00:43:40.320
and shiny and clean,

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:42.380
but no real benefit?

00:43:42.380 --> 00:43:44.960
- I think this would be an area

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:47.570
of just getting things smoother,

00:43:47.570 --> 00:43:50.180
not really changing the
age of the skin itself.

00:43:50.180 --> 00:43:51.160
But there are some treatments

00:43:51.160 --> 00:43:55.010
that probably cause enough
havoc and damage to the skin

00:43:55.010 --> 00:43:58.250
to induce minor stress, adversity.

00:43:58.250 --> 00:43:59.930
Hormesis, as we've called it.

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:01.930
And that would be microneedling.

00:44:01.930 --> 00:44:04.360
So I've never had
microneedling done, have you?

00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:06.030
- I mean, I've had tattoos.

00:44:06.030 --> 00:44:06.863
Is that the same thing?

00:44:06.863 --> 00:44:09.163
- Probably. With some bit of ink.

00:44:10.520 --> 00:44:12.450
I mean, it sounds not too pleasant,

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:14.380
but this is a machine

00:44:14.380 --> 00:44:17.430
that's putting thousands
of holes in your skin.

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:18.263
What's probably happening

00:44:18.263 --> 00:44:20.530
is that there's a hormetic effect.

00:44:20.530 --> 00:44:23.700
There's this release of
all sorts of repair factors

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:25.140
and could have long-term benefits

00:44:25.140 --> 00:44:26.380
on the age of the skin as well.

00:44:26.380 --> 00:44:28.550
But that certainly hasn't been proven yet.

00:44:28.550 --> 00:44:30.410
- Almost everything
we've talked about today

00:44:30.410 --> 00:44:31.633
has some cost involved.

00:44:32.941 --> 00:44:36.520
And there are plenty of
questions still to be resolved

00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:37.670
on a lot of these things.

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:38.503
But there's some stuff that,

00:44:38.503 --> 00:44:40.790
you know, like I call it the no dust stuff

00:44:40.790 --> 00:44:42.320
that really obvious stuff,

00:44:42.320 --> 00:44:44.420
but we should probably say it anyway.

00:44:44.420 --> 00:44:46.640
You mentioned earlier sun damage,

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:48.550
staying out of the sun's really important.

00:44:48.550 --> 00:44:51.550
Smoking is really bad for your skin.

00:44:51.550 --> 00:44:52.750
- That's probably the fastest way

00:44:52.750 --> 00:44:54.200
to accelerate aging in your body.

00:44:54.200 --> 00:44:55.550
And it shows up on your skin.

00:44:55.550 --> 00:44:56.383
There's no question.

00:44:56.383 --> 00:44:58.720
You can even see the difference in people.

00:44:58.720 --> 00:44:59.730
- A lot of people don't know this,

00:44:59.730 --> 00:45:03.210
but alcohol also shown in research

00:45:03.210 --> 00:45:04.480
to be pretty bad for our skin.

00:45:04.480 --> 00:45:06.100
So if you don't need,

00:45:06.100 --> 00:45:07.150
- Right. I didn't know that.

00:45:07.150 --> 00:45:10.980
I only recently gave up
alcohol having seen that study.

00:45:10.980 --> 00:45:13.720
- And sort of like along those same lines,

00:45:13.720 --> 00:45:15.320
smoking, alcohol, bad food,

00:45:15.320 --> 00:45:16.210
eating processed foods,

00:45:16.210 --> 00:45:18.900
eating foods that are rich
and fats and chemicals

00:45:18.900 --> 00:45:20.530
and all that gross stuff.

00:45:20.530 --> 00:45:22.730
- Yeah. It's amazing how
much your food can affect

00:45:22.730 --> 00:45:24.213
the aging of your body and your skin.

00:45:24.213 --> 00:45:26.780
And you can see the big difference
if you've ever had a pet

00:45:26.780 --> 00:45:28.490
and you change the diet
of the dog, for example.

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:30.000
We see this in our mice.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.490
We feed them good food,

00:45:31.490 --> 00:45:34.467
and their skin and their
hair changes radically.

00:45:34.467 --> 00:45:36.260
- And that's the important
transition point there.

00:45:36.260 --> 00:45:38.380
The hair also changes as well.

00:45:38.380 --> 00:45:39.350
What's good for your skin

00:45:39.350 --> 00:45:42.930
generally is good for your hair too.

00:45:42.930 --> 00:45:45.410
Let's talk about hair loss.

00:45:45.410 --> 00:45:47.460
We're also going to
talk about hair graying.

00:45:49.870 --> 00:45:53.570
Look, I started going bald
when I was in my mid-twenties.

00:45:53.570 --> 00:45:57.520
I was told to blame my
mother's side of the family.

00:45:57.520 --> 00:45:59.860
Is there any truth to
that, genetically speaking?

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:01.630
- Well, there's a little
bit of truth to that.

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:05.390
There are over 600 genes that
are involved in hair loss.

00:46:05.390 --> 00:46:08.800
And only about 20 of them are
found on the X chromosome,

00:46:08.800 --> 00:46:11.460
which is what you get from your mother.

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:14.070
Which accounts for about
a 11% of male pattern.

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:16.150
Baldness is what we're talking about here.

00:46:16.150 --> 00:46:18.880
If you're a female it's 50/50,

00:46:18.880 --> 00:46:20.580
whether it comes from your
father or your mother,

00:46:20.580 --> 00:46:23.403
because these genes are
carried on the X chromosome.

00:46:24.340 --> 00:46:25.530
So yeah, I mean, a little bit

00:46:25.530 --> 00:46:27.310
you can blame your mother's side,

00:46:27.310 --> 00:46:30.260
but most of it is fairly
random with your parents.

00:46:30.260 --> 00:46:32.030
But there's a strong genetic component.

00:46:32.030 --> 00:46:32.863
No question.

00:46:32.863 --> 00:46:34.970
It can be slightly slowed
with these treatments

00:46:34.970 --> 00:46:37.900
and modifications to your aging rate.

00:46:37.900 --> 00:46:42.070
But ultimately in this case,
it's genetically determined.

00:46:42.070 --> 00:46:43.130
That said,

00:46:43.130 --> 00:46:45.640
no one's ever died from
hell loss or hair growing.

00:46:45.640 --> 00:46:48.150
So it's not such a big issue.

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:50.170
- And actually it could be
dangerous to have long hair

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:51.110
in some instances.

00:46:51.110 --> 00:46:52.410
- Well, yeah. Even in my lab,

00:46:52.410 --> 00:46:54.080
if you have really long hair,

00:46:54.080 --> 00:46:55.170
and you look into a centrifuge,

00:46:55.170 --> 00:46:57.430
it can be quite a mess afterwards.

00:46:57.430 --> 00:47:00.490
- Also, for people who do a
lot of work with pyrotechnics,

00:47:00.490 --> 00:47:02.070
long hair, bad idea,.

00:47:02.070 --> 00:47:04.740
- Right. Says the guy who
lost his hair a while ago.

00:47:04.740 --> 00:47:07.200
- Yes. I'm safe from that.

00:47:07.200 --> 00:47:10.460
But am I aging faster than other people?

00:47:10.460 --> 00:47:12.740
I mean, is that an indicator?

00:47:12.740 --> 00:47:15.730
Again, I started losing my
hair really, really early.

00:47:15.730 --> 00:47:18.770
Is that an indicator
that I was aging early?

00:47:18.770 --> 00:47:19.680
- Not in this case.

00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:21.650
We've evolved as primates,

00:47:21.650 --> 00:47:25.610
as descendants of these
apes 6 million years ago

00:47:25.610 --> 00:47:29.600
to show off our age particularly as men.

00:47:29.600 --> 00:47:32.270
Because as we get gray, we lose our hair.

00:47:32.270 --> 00:47:34.080
We become more dominant.

00:47:34.080 --> 00:47:35.940
We seem to the rest of the tribe,

00:47:35.940 --> 00:47:37.070
we used to,

00:47:37.070 --> 00:47:39.630
that we were wiser and
we had more influence.

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:42.670
- We were stronger, we made
it through the other apes.

00:47:42.670 --> 00:47:44.100
That's why we look old.

00:47:44.100 --> 00:47:46.470
- Yeah. Well, think of
the silver back gorilla.

00:47:46.470 --> 00:47:48.130
That's the dominant one, right?

00:47:48.130 --> 00:47:48.963
Same with humans.

00:47:48.963 --> 00:47:52.490
This was a sign that you
should be given respect.

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:54.130
Not so much these days in our society.

00:47:54.130 --> 00:47:55.760
It's more about staying younger.

00:47:55.760 --> 00:47:57.510
But in previous times,

00:47:57.510 --> 00:47:59.200
even just going back a few hundred years,

00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:03.310
being gray and distinguished
was a real bonus.

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:08.310
- Okay. Why, just a fundamental
level, does hair loss occur?

00:48:10.023 --> 00:48:14.130
I mean, I understand the
evolutionary drivers,

00:48:14.130 --> 00:48:17.420
but at a cellular level, what's going on?

00:48:17.420 --> 00:48:19.450
- Well, it really goes back to stem cells.

00:48:19.450 --> 00:48:22.110
These are the cells that
keep dividing asymmetrically,

00:48:22.110 --> 00:48:23.580
giving rise to other cell types

00:48:23.580 --> 00:48:26.570
and that they reside in the
bulge of the hair follicle.

00:48:26.570 --> 00:48:28.160
And there are a variety of types.

00:48:28.160 --> 00:48:30.970
There are some that are just there

00:48:30.970 --> 00:48:32.640
to produce the keratin in the hair.

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:34.230
There are others that are there,

00:48:34.230 --> 00:48:35.740
melanocytes to produce the color.

00:48:35.740 --> 00:48:37.790
There's a new type of stem cell.

00:48:37.790 --> 00:48:39.070
It's called the HAP,

00:48:39.070 --> 00:48:41.640
the hair follicle-associated
pluripotent stem cell

00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:44.060
that people have found you can now isolate

00:48:44.060 --> 00:48:45.560
and turn into bone and muscle.

00:48:45.560 --> 00:48:47.890
We don't know what
roles they exactly play,

00:48:47.890 --> 00:48:48.780
but what happens,

00:48:48.780 --> 00:48:51.000
what we think happens during aging,

00:48:51.000 --> 00:48:53.770
and was only recently discovered
less than a few months ago,

00:48:53.770 --> 00:48:56.980
is that the important stem
cells for hair regrowth

00:48:56.980 --> 00:48:58.790
get kicked out of the hair follicle.

00:48:58.790 --> 00:49:01.000
They get spat out, which was unexpected.

00:49:01.000 --> 00:49:03.270
We thought that they died, but
they actually get expelled.

00:49:03.270 --> 00:49:05.380
- This going to like shut
out like a little can then.

00:49:05.380 --> 00:49:07.400
- Right. And there's a video of this

00:49:07.400 --> 00:49:09.163
that I tweeted about at the time.

00:49:10.020 --> 00:49:10.900
So what that means

00:49:10.900 --> 00:49:14.770
is that you want to prevent
them getting expelled.

00:49:14.770 --> 00:49:16.940
But also you want to maintain
their function as well,

00:49:16.940 --> 00:49:19.620
which is all about
preserving their epigenome,

00:49:19.620 --> 00:49:23.370
their ability to remember the
type of cell that they are.

00:49:23.370 --> 00:49:26.550
More externally, what we've
known since the 1960s,

00:49:26.550 --> 00:49:29.880
is that the he follicle
shrinks and get small,

00:49:29.880 --> 00:49:33.320
cause in part by dihydrotestosterone.

00:49:33.320 --> 00:49:34.380
And when that happens,

00:49:34.380 --> 00:49:35.730
the hair becomes thinner and thinner

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:37.450
until it stops going through

00:49:37.450 --> 00:49:39.560
what's called the Anagen Phase,

00:49:39.560 --> 00:49:41.010
which is the hair growth phase.

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:42.480
As opposed to the other three phases,

00:49:42.480 --> 00:49:45.330
which are called catagen,
telogen and exogen.

00:49:45.330 --> 00:49:47.930
- And this is really what
a lot of the treatments

00:49:47.930 --> 00:49:49.320
are aimed at preventing.

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:50.490
Let's talk about some of those.

00:49:50.490 --> 00:49:51.440
There's topical treatments.

00:49:51.440 --> 00:49:52.370
There's pills.

00:49:52.370 --> 00:49:53.203
There's some other stuff.

00:49:53.203 --> 00:49:56.220
But let's start with topical treatments.

00:49:56.220 --> 00:49:59.203
These are creams that
you rub on your head.

00:50:00.290 --> 00:50:01.650
Did these things work?

00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:03.070
- Oh, they undoubtedly worked.

00:50:03.070 --> 00:50:06.470
This is minoxidil also known as Rogaine.

00:50:06.470 --> 00:50:08.710
It was first discovered in the 1960s

00:50:08.710 --> 00:50:11.790
from a group that was trying
to lower blood pressure.

00:50:11.790 --> 00:50:15.450
So what it does is it stimulates
nitric oxide production.

00:50:15.450 --> 00:50:17.970
Nitric oxide is important in Viagra,

00:50:17.970 --> 00:50:19.420
opens up the blood vessels.

00:50:19.420 --> 00:50:20.740
And they put it into patients

00:50:20.740 --> 00:50:22.600
and they found that they
got extra hair growth.

00:50:22.600 --> 00:50:23.810
- And these were patients

00:50:23.810 --> 00:50:25.820
who were dealing with high blood pressure

00:50:25.820 --> 00:50:27.400
and their doctors
prescribed them their stuff.

00:50:27.400 --> 00:50:28.683
And they came back the next week

00:50:28.683 --> 00:50:30.050
and they're like, "What did you do to me?"

00:50:30.050 --> 00:50:31.120
- Well, a few months later, yes.

00:50:31.120 --> 00:50:32.310
Some of them got new hair.

00:50:32.310 --> 00:50:33.760
And then in the 1980s,

00:50:33.760 --> 00:50:37.440
it was formulated with an
oily substance called DMSO

00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:39.610
to get it through the
skin layer in the scalp

00:50:39.610 --> 00:50:42.190
and found that it actually
does slow down hair loss.

00:50:42.190 --> 00:50:43.870
Mostly at the back of the head,

00:50:43.870 --> 00:50:45.220
but also here.

00:50:45.220 --> 00:50:47.530
Even though it's not
recommended here, it does work

00:50:47.530 --> 00:50:49.540
if you apply it across
the whole top, like that.

00:50:49.540 --> 00:50:53.370
- Retinae also works for promoting anagen?

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:55.070
- Yeah. That's a little known fact.

00:50:55.070 --> 00:50:57.500
If you have some retinol cream, 0.5%,

00:50:57.500 --> 00:51:00.060
you can rub it on your
skin to reduce wrinkles,

00:51:00.060 --> 00:51:03.660
but you can also rub it
on the parts of your hair

00:51:03.660 --> 00:51:04.493
that are thinning out

00:51:04.493 --> 00:51:05.680
or you don't want to lose hair.

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:08.810
And especially in combination
with Rogaine, minoxidil.

00:51:08.810 --> 00:51:11.840
It works quite effectively
to slow that aging process.

00:51:11.840 --> 00:51:14.400
- Yeah. There's a study
that showed after a year,

00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:19.030
if you combine tretinoin,
which is retinae,

00:51:19.030 --> 00:51:20.770
with little bit of minoxidil,

00:51:20.770 --> 00:51:25.250
it resulted in regrowth
in 66% of the people

00:51:25.250 --> 00:51:26.220
after a year.

00:51:26.220 --> 00:51:27.053
- Oh.

00:51:27.053 --> 00:51:27.886
- That's a good result.

00:51:27.886 --> 00:51:29.530
I mean, if you're trying to regrow hair,

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:30.750
that's a really good result.

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:31.583
- Yeah, it is.

00:51:31.583 --> 00:51:32.720
- The problem with this stuff

00:51:32.720 --> 00:51:35.140
is you've got to apply
it several times a day.

00:51:35.140 --> 00:51:37.820
A lot of people don't like the
way it feels on their scalps.

00:51:37.820 --> 00:51:39.080
- Yeah. It's an oily substance.

00:51:39.080 --> 00:51:40.460
It doesn't look good, doesn't feel good.

00:51:40.460 --> 00:51:43.470
So most people stop doing that regularly.

00:51:43.470 --> 00:51:46.210
But there's an alternative
that doctors are recommending,

00:51:46.210 --> 00:51:47.480
which is taking a pill.

00:51:47.480 --> 00:51:48.590
- Propecia.
- Propecia.

00:51:48.590 --> 00:51:50.160
Also known as finasteride.

00:51:50.160 --> 00:51:51.340
Finasteride, Propecia.

00:51:51.340 --> 00:51:55.440
This is a once a day pill
that inhibits testosterone?

00:51:55.440 --> 00:51:58.664
- Well, more specifically
dihydrotestosterone.

00:51:58.664 --> 00:51:59.680
- DHT.

00:51:59.680 --> 00:52:01.943
- Right. DHT is converted
by 5-alpha reductase.

00:52:01.943 --> 00:52:04.343
It's an enzyme that's
found throughout the body.

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:08.690
Now, dihydrotestosterone
is important in the body.

00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:09.710
It reduces fat.

00:52:09.710 --> 00:52:11.960
It's good for the heart,
good for the mind.

00:52:11.960 --> 00:52:13.360
It has some other downsides.

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:14.910
It actually helps your prostate grow

00:52:14.910 --> 00:52:17.170
as you get older and you
need to go to the bathroom.

00:52:17.170 --> 00:52:18.600
So the real question is,

00:52:18.600 --> 00:52:21.940
what are the best levels
for optimal longevity?

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:24.120
And actually there was a study on that.

00:52:24.120 --> 00:52:26.130
There was a study of 3,690 men

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:29.290
that found that the levels
of that hormone were optimal

00:52:29.290 --> 00:52:31.330
if they were relatively low,

00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:35.380
but within middle range of 9.8
to 15.8 nanomoles per liter.

00:52:35.380 --> 00:52:37.660
And those were the men
that lived the longest.

00:52:37.660 --> 00:52:38.493
- Which is interesting

00:52:38.493 --> 00:52:39.870
that you're saying
there's an optimal level

00:52:39.870 --> 00:52:42.600
because there has been some
studies also looking at,

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:45.060
for instance, eunuchs,

00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:47.700
who don't have really hardly
any testosterone at all.

00:52:47.700 --> 00:52:50.720
At least not any that's been
produced by their testes.

00:52:50.720 --> 00:52:54.370
And they tend to live a
really, really long time.

00:52:54.370 --> 00:52:55.787
So on the face of it,

00:52:55.787 --> 00:52:59.010
you might think like,
"The less, the better."

00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:01.380
- Well, yeah. the testes
are doing more than

00:53:01.380 --> 00:53:02.710
putting up testosterone obviously.

00:53:02.710 --> 00:53:04.510
And cutting off testicles

00:53:04.510 --> 00:53:06.100
probably is affecting a lot of things,

00:53:06.100 --> 00:53:07.957
including the brain, depression as well.

00:53:07.957 --> 00:53:11.240
- Not by the way, a longevity
strategy that we advise.

00:53:11.240 --> 00:53:12.640
- No, we don't recommend that.

00:53:12.640 --> 00:53:14.210
Talk to your doctor first.

00:53:14.210 --> 00:53:16.450
But what's amazing about eunuchs

00:53:16.450 --> 00:53:20.450
is that they live on average
14 to 19 years longer

00:53:20.450 --> 00:53:21.912
than regular men that have deep voices.

00:53:21.912 --> 00:53:25.363
- That's better than veganism,
that's better than exercise.

00:53:27.962 --> 00:53:30.090
Like when we wrote this book,

00:53:30.090 --> 00:53:32.380
you told me that the number one thing

00:53:32.380 --> 00:53:37.380
that you learned about living
longer was eating less.

00:53:37.480 --> 00:53:38.750
- But
- I have to change that.

00:53:38.750 --> 00:53:40.110
- those numbers suggest

00:53:40.110 --> 00:53:42.860
there is actually something
else people could do.

00:53:42.860 --> 00:53:45.330
- Right. Cut off your
balls, if you're a man.

00:53:45.330 --> 00:53:46.230
- But don't do that.

00:53:46.230 --> 00:53:47.080
- Don't do that.

00:53:47.080 --> 00:53:48.370
But it's interesting

00:53:48.370 --> 00:53:51.070
that eunuchs tend to
live a really long time

00:53:51.070 --> 00:53:52.240
as do smaller people

00:53:52.240 --> 00:53:56.320
so the excess of testosterone
and growth hormone in general

00:53:56.320 --> 00:54:01.030
seems to be promoting an
abundance memetic, okay?

00:54:01.030 --> 00:54:03.490
But what's really cool
about these eunuchs is that,

00:54:03.490 --> 00:54:04.690
I looked up the numbers,

00:54:05.885 --> 00:54:08.930
out of a bunch of eunuchs, 81 in total,

00:54:08.930 --> 00:54:11.530
three of them became
centenarians, lived to a hundred,

00:54:11.530 --> 00:54:14.080
which is unheard of in
the normal population.

00:54:14.080 --> 00:54:16.690
Normally, the chance in the
US of reaching a hundred

00:54:16.690 --> 00:54:19.189
is only 1 in 4,400 people.

00:54:19.189 --> 00:54:21.067
- So it's one in 4,400.

00:54:21.067 --> 00:54:23.390
And in the eunuchs, it was 1 in 30.

00:54:23.390 --> 00:54:24.450
Greater than 1 in 30.

00:54:24.450 --> 00:54:26.930
- Yeah, exactly. That's
a 130 times greater

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:29.090
than the background rate, the normal rate.

00:54:29.090 --> 00:54:30.620
So clearly it works.

00:54:30.620 --> 00:54:33.430
It's just not something
you'd want to live with

00:54:33.430 --> 00:54:34.263
on a daily basis.

00:54:34.263 --> 00:54:35.880
- This might extend your life.

00:54:35.880 --> 00:54:39.880
Like it'll make your life
longer, but not better.

00:54:39.880 --> 00:54:41.190
- Well, and if it doesn't work,

00:54:41.190 --> 00:54:42.403
it'll just feel longer.

00:54:43.900 --> 00:54:46.320
- This really relates
to one of the problems

00:54:46.320 --> 00:54:47.710
with inhibited testosterone,

00:54:47.710 --> 00:54:49.410
which is that it lowers sex drive.

00:54:49.410 --> 00:54:51.600
That's a potential side
effect to Propecia?

00:54:51.600 --> 00:54:55.670
- Yeah. There's a bunch,
including mood swings.

00:54:55.670 --> 00:54:58.670
Well, you can be depressed
and breast tenderness as well.

00:54:58.670 --> 00:54:59.503
- Yeah.

00:54:59.503 --> 00:55:02.920
You really have to just
hate the idea of being bald

00:55:02.920 --> 00:55:06.590
to go through or to subject
yourself to a lot of this stuff.

00:55:06.590 --> 00:55:08.380
- Well, I disagree.

00:55:08.380 --> 00:55:09.780
Because these are side effects

00:55:09.780 --> 00:55:11.700
that are in a low number of people.

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:14.717
So you could try it if you
want to see how it affects you.

00:55:14.717 --> 00:55:16.477
And if it's fine, then good to go.

00:55:16.477 --> 00:55:17.970
But make sure you tell your doctor

00:55:17.970 --> 00:55:19.120
if there's something else going on.

00:55:19.120 --> 00:55:21.600
- There's another intervention that has,

00:55:21.600 --> 00:55:24.700
as far as we can tell, almost
no side effects whatsoever.

00:55:24.700 --> 00:55:29.020
It sounds super cheesy and futuristic,

00:55:29.020 --> 00:55:31.290
fricking laser beams.

00:55:31.290 --> 00:55:32.123
- Yeah. Laser beams.

00:55:32.123 --> 00:55:33.470
I didn't think this was real.

00:55:33.470 --> 00:55:34.303
It sounds crazy.

00:55:34.303 --> 00:55:38.290
You shine light on your head
and your hair will grow again.

00:55:38.290 --> 00:55:39.180
Come on.

00:55:39.180 --> 00:55:40.013
But I looked into it

00:55:40.013 --> 00:55:43.350
and it's actually been approved
by the FDA as a treatment.

00:55:43.350 --> 00:55:45.830
There are combs that have red laser light,

00:55:45.830 --> 00:55:47.420
and caps that you can wear.

00:55:47.420 --> 00:55:48.470
These are typically treatments

00:55:48.470 --> 00:55:51.250
for 10, 15 minutes a
day or every other day.

00:55:51.250 --> 00:55:54.140
And they literally have been
proven, clinical trials,

00:55:54.140 --> 00:55:55.470
to slow down hair loss,

00:55:55.470 --> 00:55:59.050
as well as regrow some aspects
of hair in, not everybody,

00:55:59.050 --> 00:56:00.410
but in the majority of people.

00:56:00.410 --> 00:56:02.053
- And this is called LLLT?

00:56:03.560 --> 00:56:05.760
- Low laser light therapy.

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:06.890
- Therapy. I think that's right.

00:56:06.890 --> 00:56:10.930
And we're not quite sure how this works.

00:56:10.930 --> 00:56:14.100
We know it works, we're
not quite sure how.

00:56:14.100 --> 00:56:16.460
There's a leading theory
about mitochondria.

00:56:16.460 --> 00:56:17.730
- Yeah.

00:56:17.730 --> 00:56:19.140
Some people think it's due to heat.

00:56:19.140 --> 00:56:20.860
I think that that's a bunch of BS.

00:56:20.860 --> 00:56:23.300
What probably is happening
that makes sense to me

00:56:23.300 --> 00:56:26.490
is we've heard about nitric oxide earlier

00:56:26.490 --> 00:56:30.330
about opening up the arteries
and the blood vessels.

00:56:30.330 --> 00:56:33.160
Nitric oxide also controls
mitochondrial function.

00:56:33.160 --> 00:56:34.870
And that laser light

00:56:34.870 --> 00:56:39.870
has been shown to dissociate
this nitric oxide enzyme from,

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:42.140
what we talked about
earlier in another episode,

00:56:42.140 --> 00:56:44.420
the electron transport chain.

00:56:44.420 --> 00:56:46.630
There are proteins that make the energy.

00:56:46.630 --> 00:56:48.320
They're called the
electron transport chain.

00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:49.840
There are five of those,

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:51.890
and they're made up of multiple proteins.

00:56:51.890 --> 00:56:54.690
And number five gets
disrupted by laser light.

00:56:54.690 --> 00:56:57.350
In fact, this nitric oxide, synthase,

00:56:57.350 --> 00:56:58.870
which is a protein that
we mentioned earlier

00:56:58.870 --> 00:57:01.360
for blood vessels, also plays a role here.

00:57:01.360 --> 00:57:03.900
And the laser light
takes away that protein,

00:57:03.900 --> 00:57:06.310
that nitric oxide, synthase moves away.

00:57:06.310 --> 00:57:08.350
And now what you get is
a bunch of free radicals

00:57:08.350 --> 00:57:09.920
in the mitochondrion,

00:57:09.920 --> 00:57:12.020
which results in what
we call mitohormesis,

00:57:12.020 --> 00:57:14.260
A little bit of what doesn't
kill your mitochondria

00:57:14.260 --> 00:57:16.220
makes them stronger and multiply.

00:57:16.220 --> 00:57:17.720
And probably that increase in energy

00:57:17.720 --> 00:57:19.440
is what allows those stem cells

00:57:19.440 --> 00:57:21.420
to stay young and rejuvenate.

00:57:21.420 --> 00:57:23.590
- Like rejuvenating stem cells,

00:57:23.590 --> 00:57:26.680
which means that all of these strategies

00:57:26.680 --> 00:57:30.383
for addressing balding,

00:57:31.860 --> 00:57:33.760
the creams, the pills,

00:57:33.760 --> 00:57:35.420
the one that's actually having

00:57:35.420 --> 00:57:39.860
potentially seems to be having
an actual anti-aging effect

00:57:39.860 --> 00:57:41.610
is the fricking laser beams.

00:57:41.610 --> 00:57:43.180
- It is funny the world we live in.

00:57:43.180 --> 00:57:45.120
But it's beyond hair now

00:57:45.120 --> 00:57:47.230
that it looks like
shining red laser light,

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:49.050
which can penetrate deeply into the skin,

00:57:49.050 --> 00:57:50.540
even into the sub layers

00:57:50.540 --> 00:57:52.560
can be beneficial on the entire body.

00:57:52.560 --> 00:57:55.890
And this is why we're seeing
a huge increase in the sales

00:57:55.890 --> 00:58:00.150
of saunas that have infrared
laser light as well.

00:58:00.150 --> 00:58:04.840
- Let's talk about
platelet-rich plasma injection.

00:58:04.840 --> 00:58:06.540
Another thing that's gaining popularity

00:58:06.540 --> 00:58:08.280
for a variety of conditions.

00:58:08.280 --> 00:58:09.910
A lot of athletes use these

00:58:09.910 --> 00:58:13.560
to recuperate after sports injuries,

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:16.030
but people are increasingly using these

00:58:16.030 --> 00:58:17.970
for hair loss as well.

00:58:17.970 --> 00:58:21.497
- They are. It's a
platelet-rich plasma, PRP.

00:58:21.497 --> 00:58:25.130
And what you get done
is you go into a clinic,

00:58:25.130 --> 00:58:26.020
they'll take out your blood,

00:58:26.020 --> 00:58:29.750
they'll spin out the cells,
take the platelet-rich plasma,

00:58:29.750 --> 00:58:32.160
and inject it either into
your veins for longevity.

00:58:32.160 --> 00:58:35.410
Or in most cases, put it into your scalp

00:58:35.410 --> 00:58:38.050
in many different places with a needle.

00:58:38.050 --> 00:58:40.700
They anesthetised it so
that you don't cry too much.

00:58:40.700 --> 00:58:42.300
And what is probably happening

00:58:42.300 --> 00:58:45.640
is that this plasma is filled
with a bunch of goodies.

00:58:45.640 --> 00:58:47.350
Exosomes we've talked about before,

00:58:47.350 --> 00:58:49.370
hormones, little peptides,

00:58:49.370 --> 00:58:51.220
probably what are called VSELL,

00:58:51.220 --> 00:58:52.860
very small embryonic like cells.

00:58:52.860 --> 00:58:55.330
We're get to that in another episode.

00:58:55.330 --> 00:58:56.820
This mixture of things

00:58:56.820 --> 00:59:00.500
probably helps the stem
cells in the follicle

00:59:00.500 --> 00:59:03.130
get rejuvenated, maybe even
turn back the clock on those.

00:59:03.130 --> 00:59:06.160
But exactly what component
in there that's working?

00:59:06.160 --> 00:59:07.660
We really have no idea.

00:59:07.660 --> 00:59:10.500
- You know the point of
frustration in irony,

00:59:10.500 --> 00:59:13.790
which is that blood banks
and hospitals systems

00:59:13.790 --> 00:59:16.800
are trying to get people
to donate platelets,

00:59:16.800 --> 00:59:20.050
for lifesaving research and therapies.

00:59:20.050 --> 00:59:21.380
But one good way to get people

00:59:21.380 --> 00:59:23.090
to get their platelets out of their body

00:59:23.090 --> 00:59:25.080
is to tell 'em that they
can regrow their hair.

00:59:25.080 --> 00:59:25.913
- Yeah, it's true.

00:59:25.913 --> 00:59:27.050
There are a lot of people
who don't just care

00:59:27.050 --> 00:59:28.700
about feeling good and living longer,

00:59:28.700 --> 00:59:30.190
but also how good they look,

00:59:30.190 --> 00:59:32.260
and are willing to spend
a lot of money on that.

00:59:32.260 --> 00:59:34.610
- Is there an anti-aging pathway

00:59:34.610 --> 00:59:36.293
involved in the PRP, do you think?

00:59:36.293 --> 00:59:40.380
I mean, you noted these like
the exosomes and the peptides,

00:59:40.380 --> 00:59:42.540
these are all things we
talked about last episode.

00:59:42.540 --> 00:59:43.950
It sounds like potentially.

00:59:43.950 --> 00:59:45.500
- Well, undoubtedly.

00:59:45.500 --> 00:59:47.570
We know that when you
fuse the blood systems,

00:59:47.570 --> 00:59:49.830
the circulatory systems of an
old mouse and a young mouse,

00:59:49.830 --> 00:59:54.590
the young mouse makes the old
mouse rejuvenated and younger.

00:59:54.590 --> 00:59:56.120
We don't know all the components in there.

00:59:56.120 --> 00:59:56.953
There are some of them.

00:59:56.953 --> 00:59:59.890
There's one called GDF15, for example,

00:59:59.890 --> 01:00:01.960
but we don't of what's in this mixture

01:00:01.960 --> 01:00:03.380
that promotes hair growth.

01:00:03.380 --> 01:00:05.350
When it's found, it'll be purified

01:00:05.350 --> 01:00:08.370
and probably be given as
a cream or an injectable.

01:00:08.370 --> 01:00:11.690
But until then, it's
this messy PRP treatment.

01:00:11.690 --> 01:00:16.640
- Before we move on
from balding to graying,

01:00:16.640 --> 01:00:19.440
there's this sort of like hair thing

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:22.160
that a lot of people are
familiar with when they age

01:00:22.160 --> 01:00:25.180
and that is it stops growing on your head,

01:00:25.180 --> 01:00:26.750
it starts growing in other places.

01:00:26.750 --> 01:00:27.640
You get in your nose.

01:00:27.640 --> 01:00:29.060
Your eyebrow hair start to get long.

01:00:29.060 --> 01:00:31.010
You get in your ears.

01:00:31.010 --> 01:00:32.230
What's going on here?

01:00:32.230 --> 01:00:33.090
- We don't really know

01:00:33.090 --> 01:00:35.170
why hair starts growing
in the wrong places,

01:00:35.170 --> 01:00:36.840
as annoying as it is.

01:00:36.840 --> 01:00:38.550
Probably I think what's happening

01:00:38.550 --> 01:00:40.550
is that we have this evolutionary program

01:00:40.550 --> 01:00:42.270
where we used to be a lot hairier,

01:00:42.270 --> 01:00:44.030
six to 10 million years ago.

01:00:44.030 --> 01:00:46.950
And those stem cells are
still ready to grow thick hair

01:00:46.950 --> 01:00:48.770
in our ears, on our nose,

01:00:48.770 --> 01:00:51.240
wherever you don't want it to grow.

01:00:51.240 --> 01:00:55.240
And that the changes in
the structure of the DNA,

01:00:55.240 --> 01:00:57.320
what we call the epigenome,

01:00:57.320 --> 01:00:58.630
is changing over time.

01:00:58.630 --> 01:01:01.350
And that those regions
that are normally silent,

01:01:01.350 --> 01:01:02.183
in the ears.

01:01:02.183 --> 01:01:03.480
So you don't get hairy ears,

01:01:03.480 --> 01:01:05.610
are unraveling as part
of the aging process.

01:01:05.610 --> 01:01:07.910
- So these are parts
of ancient genetic code

01:01:10.270 --> 01:01:13.720
that have been allowed to
escape from the histones,

01:01:13.720 --> 01:01:15.530
and now they're readable by the cells.

01:01:15.530 --> 01:01:17.950
- Right. And we become our
ancestors, unfortunately.

01:01:17.950 --> 01:01:20.473
So shave or pluck that out for now.

01:01:21.450 --> 01:01:22.980
What that means is we would predict

01:01:22.980 --> 01:01:24.550
that if we can slow down aging

01:01:24.550 --> 01:01:26.900
using the methods that
we've talked about in this

01:01:26.900 --> 01:01:28.170
and other episodes,

01:01:28.170 --> 01:01:30.740
we should also prevent that
process from happening,

01:01:30.740 --> 01:01:33.320
or at least delay it
till much later in life.

01:01:33.320 --> 01:01:35.880
- Would that be the case
with graying hair too?

01:01:35.880 --> 01:01:36.713
- It could be.

01:01:36.713 --> 01:01:39.580
Because graying is part of
not just a genetic program,

01:01:39.580 --> 01:01:41.210
but can be accelerated

01:01:41.210 --> 01:01:44.270
by things that are also known
to accelerate aging itself,

01:01:44.270 --> 01:01:46.393
such as psychological stress.

01:01:47.523 --> 01:01:48.570
- This is really interesting

01:01:48.570 --> 01:01:51.350
because we know from some
fairly recent research

01:01:51.350 --> 01:01:52.680
that stress plays a key.

01:01:52.680 --> 01:01:53.907
I mean, it's always been sort of known,

01:01:53.907 --> 01:01:55.330
"Oh, you're going to
make me go gray." Right?

01:01:55.330 --> 01:01:56.210
My grandmother, "You're
going to make me go gray."

01:01:56.210 --> 01:01:58.700
And I'll be like, "Grandma,
you're already gray."

01:01:58.700 --> 01:02:01.290
But we've long known

01:02:01.290 --> 01:02:03.970
that gray hair is associated with stress.

01:02:03.970 --> 01:02:05.810
What's coming out now

01:02:05.810 --> 01:02:08.230
is that it doesn't have to be permanent.

01:02:08.230 --> 01:02:10.030
- It's been known for probably centuries

01:02:10.030 --> 01:02:11.710
that you can have these
binary colored hairs,

01:02:11.710 --> 01:02:14.150
where at the tip of the hair, it's dark.

01:02:14.150 --> 01:02:15.210
And then it's gray in the middle,

01:02:15.210 --> 01:02:16.750
and then dark again at the bottom.

01:02:16.750 --> 01:02:18.440
And people have wondered
what the heck is going on.

01:02:18.440 --> 01:02:20.440
And just recently in 2021,

01:02:20.440 --> 01:02:21.740
a group of researchers

01:02:21.740 --> 01:02:23.610
had a look at what was
happening in people's lives

01:02:23.610 --> 01:02:26.680
during that gray hair growth period.

01:02:26.680 --> 01:02:29.180
And they found that they were
remarkably stressful periods

01:02:29.180 --> 01:02:30.130
of those people's lives

01:02:30.130 --> 01:02:32.340
where they didn't stop working,

01:02:32.340 --> 01:02:34.940
they didn't sleep, they
didn't go on a vacation.

01:02:34.940 --> 01:02:36.530
And so I think it's very clear

01:02:36.530 --> 01:02:39.423
that stress can induce gray hair,

01:02:39.423 --> 01:02:41.250
a loss of color from the hair.

01:02:41.250 --> 01:02:43.360
But what's also remarkable
about that finding

01:02:43.360 --> 01:02:46.640
is that it proves that
gray hair is reversible.

01:02:46.640 --> 01:02:48.930
- Which means that what
we're talking about here

01:02:48.930 --> 01:02:50.630
is an epigenetic effect.

01:02:50.630 --> 01:02:52.740
- Sure. I mean, anything that is genetic

01:02:52.740 --> 01:02:54.390
is essentially irreversible.

01:02:54.390 --> 01:02:56.960
So this is an epigenetic effect.

01:02:56.960 --> 01:02:57.793
What I would imagine

01:02:57.793 --> 01:03:00.010
is that after you've been
for many, many years,

01:03:00.010 --> 01:03:03.700
it's going to be very
difficult to reverse that.

01:03:03.700 --> 01:03:04.810
But in the early phases,

01:03:04.810 --> 01:03:06.450
when you're getting this spattering

01:03:06.450 --> 01:03:08.440
of gray and color, gray and color,

01:03:08.440 --> 01:03:11.700
you are able to get those packages of DNA

01:03:11.700 --> 01:03:13.290
back to where they were
when you were young

01:03:13.290 --> 01:03:15.770
using some of the methods that
we're talking about today.

01:03:15.770 --> 01:03:17.440
And we've talked about in other episodes.

01:03:17.440 --> 01:03:18.900
- And this has to do with those stem cells

01:03:18.900 --> 01:03:19.770
that produce pigment.

01:03:19.770 --> 01:03:21.870
They're melanocytes?

01:03:21.870 --> 01:03:22.703
- Yep.

01:03:22.703 --> 01:03:24.320
- And these sit right
next to our hair shafts.

01:03:24.320 --> 01:03:26.200
- They do. And they inject the color

01:03:26.200 --> 01:03:30.020
as the keratin is being put
together into that hair shaft.

01:03:30.020 --> 01:03:32.370
And the prevailing theory
as to why we get gray

01:03:32.370 --> 01:03:33.700
is that these melanocytes die

01:03:33.700 --> 01:03:35.653
through a process called apoptosis.

01:03:36.570 --> 01:03:37.810
Hopefully that isn't true.

01:03:37.810 --> 01:03:39.270
I think it's true for very late in life.

01:03:39.270 --> 01:03:41.310
But what we're seeing in this new study

01:03:41.310 --> 01:03:44.070
is that they become
dysfunctional before they die.

01:03:44.070 --> 01:03:46.510
And that's a period that we have a chance

01:03:46.510 --> 01:03:49.690
to recover their function
and prevent them from dying.

01:03:49.690 --> 01:03:50.560
And there are a number of ways

01:03:50.560 --> 01:03:52.210
that I could think of at least,

01:03:52.210 --> 01:03:55.210
to reverse that and
prevent them from dying.

01:03:55.210 --> 01:03:56.240
One way though,

01:03:56.240 --> 01:03:58.887
would be to use some of
these adversity mimetics

01:03:58.887 --> 01:04:01.560
to get that epigenome to reset.

01:04:01.560 --> 01:04:04.760
- That's what some researchers
have done in mice, at least,

01:04:04.760 --> 01:04:07.900
in a fairly recent study,
actually this year,

01:04:07.900 --> 01:04:10.490
using a combination compound.

01:04:10.490 --> 01:04:12.560
Including cyclosporine,

01:04:12.560 --> 01:04:14.200
a minoxidil,

01:04:14.200 --> 01:04:16.720
which is the cream that
we talked about earlier,

01:04:16.720 --> 01:04:18.920
and then another pigment-promoting drug.

01:04:18.920 --> 01:04:20.310
We dug into this a little today,

01:04:20.310 --> 01:04:21.980
and got really excited by what we found.

01:04:21.980 --> 01:04:23.950
- Yeah. Because the paper tries to obscure

01:04:23.950 --> 01:04:25.410
what this actual chemical is.

01:04:25.410 --> 01:04:29.960
This age-reversing and
pigment-promoting drug.

01:04:29.960 --> 01:04:31.810
Turns out it's called tacrolimus,

01:04:31.810 --> 01:04:35.360
which is a very similar
molecule to rapamycin

01:04:35.360 --> 01:04:36.970
or also known as sirolimus,

01:04:36.970 --> 01:04:39.010
which we've mentioned in earlier episodes

01:04:39.010 --> 01:04:41.410
is one of the main drugs
that can extend lifespan

01:04:41.410 --> 01:04:44.360
and inhibit this complex
of proteins called mTOR

01:04:44.360 --> 01:04:45.830
that responds to fasting.

01:04:45.830 --> 01:04:49.120
- And like cyclosporine,
it's an immunosuppressant.

01:04:49.120 --> 01:04:52.200
Which means it's an adversity mimetics.

01:04:52.200 --> 01:04:53.960
It's showing your cells

01:04:53.960 --> 01:04:55.560
that times are not all
that great right now.

01:04:55.560 --> 01:04:57.980
- Right. It's making
your stem cells freak out

01:04:57.980 --> 01:04:59.150
that things are going to be rough.

01:04:59.150 --> 01:05:01.080
And maybe we should be rejuvenate

01:05:01.080 --> 01:05:02.220
and start growing a little better.

01:05:02.220 --> 01:05:04.370
I just want to mention
this cyclosporine A,

01:05:04.370 --> 01:05:05.203
it's really interesting.

01:05:05.203 --> 01:05:06.410
You said it's an immunosuppressant.

01:05:06.410 --> 01:05:09.310
It's used to prevent organ rejection.

01:05:09.310 --> 01:05:12.930
In my lab, we found it also
rejuvenates mitochondria

01:05:12.930 --> 01:05:15.280
through actually making sure what's called

01:05:15.280 --> 01:05:19.380
the mitochondrial permeability
transition pore or MPTP

01:05:19.380 --> 01:05:20.490
is preserved.

01:05:20.490 --> 01:05:21.323
Long story short,

01:05:21.323 --> 01:05:23.640
I think this combination of cyclosporin A

01:05:23.640 --> 01:05:25.123
for mitochondrial activity,

01:05:25.970 --> 01:05:29.810
minoxidil, which we talked
about, improved blood flow.

01:05:29.810 --> 01:05:31.690
And this pigment-promoting drug,

01:05:31.690 --> 01:05:34.520
which is basically an analog of rapamycin,

01:05:34.520 --> 01:05:36.880
which simulate a fasting response,

01:05:36.880 --> 01:05:40.880
is the triple combo for
hair repigmentation.

01:05:40.880 --> 01:05:43.100
- This is not yet ready for human use.

01:05:43.100 --> 01:05:44.620
Undoubtedly, there's probably somebody

01:05:44.620 --> 01:05:45.940
trying this out in Hollywood.

01:05:45.940 --> 01:05:50.940
But as of right now, this is
still sometime into the future.

01:05:50.950 --> 01:05:51.900
- I think so.

01:05:51.900 --> 01:05:54.970
You know, people are already
trying rapamycin as a drug,

01:05:54.970 --> 01:05:57.190
10 milligrams every week or so.

01:05:57.190 --> 01:05:59.370
This is only being done by a few people

01:05:59.370 --> 01:06:00.860
under doctor supervision,

01:06:00.860 --> 01:06:03.400
but I could imagine that
there will be products

01:06:03.400 --> 01:06:05.720
made available to the
general public one day

01:06:05.720 --> 01:06:07.570
that would definitely restore hair color.

01:06:07.570 --> 01:06:09.620
It's not a miracle that this happens.

01:06:09.620 --> 01:06:11.280
It's just science and we're
going to figure it out.

01:06:11.280 --> 01:06:13.740
- And just like another one of those cases

01:06:13.740 --> 01:06:17.840
where when we address an aging pathway,

01:06:17.840 --> 01:06:20.150
we're addressing things
that are downstream

01:06:20.150 --> 01:06:21.330
of that aging pathway.

01:06:21.330 --> 01:06:22.820
And this really relates,

01:06:22.820 --> 01:06:25.830
I think, really well to a general theme

01:06:25.830 --> 01:06:28.040
of what we've been talking about
over the past few episodes,

01:06:28.040 --> 01:06:32.130
which is probably the individual problems

01:06:32.130 --> 01:06:33.717
that come with aging that
we talk about right now

01:06:33.717 --> 01:06:35.380
are not the things that
need to be addressed

01:06:35.380 --> 01:06:37.260
if we can hit aging

01:06:37.260 --> 01:06:39.310
somewhere up to stream of those problems.

01:06:39.310 --> 01:06:41.340
- Yeah. And another important point is

01:06:41.340 --> 01:06:44.940
that when we learn how to
reverse aging in the skin

01:06:44.940 --> 01:06:47.740
and rejuvenate the hair and
get it to produce more color,

01:06:47.740 --> 01:06:50.890
those lessons can be
applied across the body.

01:06:50.890 --> 01:06:54.200
Because all cells have a
fundamental root cause of aging

01:06:54.200 --> 01:06:56.970
and the same defense pathways
against that process.

01:06:56.970 --> 01:07:00.990
So for instance, rapamycin,
cyclosporine, minoxidil,

01:07:00.990 --> 01:07:04.340
these could be used
perhaps in really low doses

01:07:04.340 --> 01:07:05.640
and under clinical conditions,

01:07:05.640 --> 01:07:08.030
tested to see if they
have rejuvenating effects

01:07:08.030 --> 01:07:09.350
in other organs as well.

01:07:09.350 --> 01:07:10.750
- So maybe right now,

01:07:10.750 --> 01:07:12.150
we're trying to figure out

01:07:12.150 --> 01:07:15.710
how to help people keep
their hair more colorful,

01:07:15.710 --> 01:07:19.080
try to keep it in their scalp
rather than on the floor.

01:07:19.080 --> 01:07:20.850
But sometime down the road,

01:07:20.850 --> 01:07:22.330
we could be addressing things

01:07:22.330 --> 01:07:26.880
that are far greater
importance to far more people.

01:07:26.880 --> 01:07:27.713
- Right,

01:07:27.713 --> 01:07:31.270
And we foresee a day in my
lab where you can take a pill,

01:07:31.270 --> 01:07:32.600
maybe three pills,

01:07:32.600 --> 01:07:35.350
and you'll not just get younger,
but your hair will regrow

01:07:35.350 --> 01:07:37.160
and become dark again if you wanted to.

01:07:37.160 --> 01:07:38.940
- So we've covered a lot today.

01:07:38.940 --> 01:07:40.760
As always, if people want to go back,

01:07:40.760 --> 01:07:42.240
they can use the show notes,

01:07:42.240 --> 01:07:43.480
which are timestamped

01:07:43.480 --> 01:07:45.570
to find the thing that
they want to talk about.

01:07:45.570 --> 01:07:47.790
Just gimme a big picture here though,

01:07:47.790 --> 01:07:50.090
of everything that we've talked about

01:07:50.090 --> 01:07:52.270
and what's the big takeaway today?

01:07:52.270 --> 01:07:53.200
- Well, the big takeaway

01:07:53.200 --> 01:07:55.810
is that we're on the verge of
being able to reverse aging

01:07:55.810 --> 01:07:59.480
in the body internally,
but also externally.

01:07:59.480 --> 01:08:02.370
And that'll be a world where
people not just live longer,

01:08:02.370 --> 01:08:04.650
but feel better about themselves as well.

01:08:04.650 --> 01:08:07.890
We've talked about injectables,
edibles, smearables.

01:08:07.890 --> 01:08:10.970
It's a lot of ways of getting
these molecules into the body.

01:08:10.970 --> 01:08:12.000
Not just ingesting them,

01:08:12.000 --> 01:08:13.620
but putting them on the surface

01:08:13.620 --> 01:08:14.700
and getting them into the layers

01:08:14.700 --> 01:08:17.430
where they needed to slow
down or reverse aging.

01:08:17.430 --> 01:08:18.670
And there's a lot to talk about

01:08:18.670 --> 01:08:21.700
about the future of
all of this development

01:08:21.700 --> 01:08:22.900
and what we're going to be seeing

01:08:22.900 --> 01:08:24.510
in the next 5 to 10 years as well,

01:08:24.510 --> 01:08:26.230
which we'll cover in a later episode.

01:08:26.230 --> 01:08:27.220
- At the top of the show,

01:08:27.220 --> 01:08:29.490
I mentioned that I had
come into this topic

01:08:29.490 --> 01:08:30.760
a little bit reluctantly.

01:08:30.760 --> 01:08:33.450
I didn't think we really
needed to be spinning our time,

01:08:33.450 --> 01:08:34.287
helping people.

01:08:34.287 --> 01:08:35.780
- You thought it was a superficial topic.

01:08:35.780 --> 01:08:37.520
- I did. I thought it
was a superficial topic

01:08:37.520 --> 01:08:40.260
and I can admit when I'm wrong.

01:08:40.260 --> 01:08:41.243
- Rarely, but yeah.

01:08:42.110 --> 01:08:44.070
- Once in a while.

01:08:44.070 --> 01:08:47.950
This isn't just about looking good.

01:08:47.950 --> 01:08:49.593
This is part of the
progression of the things

01:08:49.593 --> 01:08:51.560
that we've been talking about

01:08:51.560 --> 01:08:54.350
throughout the course of this series.

01:08:54.350 --> 01:08:55.560
None of this negates.

01:08:55.560 --> 01:08:58.580
Also the importance of doing the exercise

01:08:58.580 --> 01:09:00.000
and eating well,

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:03.960
and engaging the circuits in your body

01:09:03.960 --> 01:09:05.750
that respond to adversity.

01:09:05.750 --> 01:09:07.140
- Getting enough sleep, don't stress,

01:09:07.140 --> 01:09:07.973
all of that stuff we talked about.

01:09:07.973 --> 01:09:09.460
- All that stuff is important.

01:09:09.460 --> 01:09:12.330
But then the outcome of that

01:09:12.330 --> 01:09:15.440
is it can be that you look better.

01:09:15.440 --> 01:09:17.010
That's okay.

01:09:17.010 --> 01:09:19.590
- Right. So the moral of this episode is,

01:09:19.590 --> 01:09:21.270
if you can keep yourself looking good

01:09:21.270 --> 01:09:22.900
by doing the right things,

01:09:22.900 --> 01:09:25.530
